{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AwNhMhFcwe11uPN8VQq1WM","type":"tool_result","content":"420\t### 4.1 Rail3D, the renderer\n421\t**Data**\n422\t- **Mesh**: `{ groups: [{ mat, pos: Float32Array, nrm, uv, lit: Float32Array (baked light per vertex), flags }], bounds, sphere }`.\n423\t- **TextureBank** (built at boot):\n424\t  - each material has 5 band tiles: `E.materialTile(id, { size: 32, px: 2, band: 0..4 })`, plus `lid: true` versions\n425\t    for faces that look up or down. Each tile is converted to a `Uint32Array`.\n426\t  - Tiles are `v`-up with the top row first, so flip v when sampling.\n427\t  - Face-pattern materials (crate) use uv 0..1 across each face.\n428\t  - Use **named** materials in shape entries so `materialTile(id)` resolves them. Register any inline material in\n429\t    `E.MATERIALS` under a generated id first.\n430\t  - Material flags: `glow` (emissive), `water`, `double` (draw both sides), `alphaTest` (nets, grates, foliage cards,\n431\t    on the tile's alpha or a pattern mask), `scroll` (uv speed).\n432\t- **Instances**: `{ mesh, M (3×4), visible, dynamic, tint, flash, collider ids }`.\n433\t- **Static world**: built when a stage is built, then transformed to world space and baked. The baked light is\n434\t  `ambient + sunK·max(0, n·sun) + Σ static lights` plus contact darkening where a wall meets the floor. Tessellate\n435\t  faces to edges of 64 u or less so the vertex light, fog and near clipping stay clean. Bin it into 256-u chunks with\n436\t  bounding spheres for frustum culling.\n437\t\n438\t**Per viewport, per frame**\n439\t1. `begin(cam, rect)`: the basis, `F`, the frustum planes; clear the depth to `+∞`.\n440\t2. **Sky**, per pixel from the ray's elevation e and azimuth a:\n441\t   - a three-stop gradient (the stage palette);\n442\t   - stars from a hash of the quantized direction where e > 0.05, twinkling;\n443\t   - a moon or sun disc with a halo;\n444\t   - two octaves of drifting noise clouds;\n445\t   - one to three distant silhouette layers on the horizon (`h(a)` heightfields: skyline, cliffs, the barrier);\n446\t   - lightning brightens it.\n447\t3. **Triangles**, for each visible group:\n448\t   - transform to view space, clip against the near plane (z = 2 u; Sutherland–Hodgman, a triangle becomes 0–2),\n449\t     project, and cull back faces unless the material is `double`;\n450\t   - rasterize over the integer bounding box with edge functions and a top-left fill rule. Interpolate `1/z`, `u/z`,\n451\t     `v/z` and `L/z` for perspective-correct values;\n452\t   - depth test on view z;\n453\t   - per pixel: `band = clamp(floor(L·4 + bayer4[x&3][y&3]), 0, 4)` (the engine's banded light, dithered between\n454\t     bands) → texel → fog → write color and depth;\n455\t   - fog `f = clamp((z − near)/(far − near))^1.2`, quantized to 8 steps with the Bayer threshold. Emissive materials\n456\t     skip lighting and take half the fog.\n457\t   - *Retro wobble* (a setting, off by default) snaps vertices to whole pixels and makes texturing affine.\n458\t4. **Dynamic light** on top of the baked light: up to 12 point lights per frame (muzzle flashes, explosions, flares,\n459\t   the lighthouse beam, the tram's lamps). Each adds `k·(1 − d/r)²` per vertex, only for triangles whose bounding\n460\t   sphere it touches.\n461\t5. **Water**: a material with `water: true`.\n462\t   - The band comes from `base + 0.6·sin(u·.11 + t·1.3)·sin(v·.07 − t)` plus noise.\n463\t   - Sparkles: the `hi` color where noise(u + t·8, v) > 0.82, weighted toward the moon or sun direction.\n464\t   - Vertex foam near shores and sluices.\n465\t   - Anything that falls below the water height spawns a splash.\n466\t6. **Actors as billboards.** Sort far to near; for each actor:\n467\t   - project the foot point; `k = F/z`; skip it if it is under 3 px tall or off screen.\n468\t   - Choose the view with `new E.View('r3', 'Rail3D', yawDeg, pitchDeg, k, 1)`. Take `yawDeg` from the\n469\t     **camera→actor bearing** `b`: `−b·180/π − 90`, as `E.Mode7.view` does from the heading. Take `pitchDeg` from the\n470\t     ray's downward elevation, **clamped to at least 3°** (a View at 1° or less becomes a side view, and Views can't\n471\t     look up). Cache the views by quantized key: yaw and pitch in 2° steps, k in 1/16 steps.\n472\t   - Clear a scratch canvas sized to the actor's bounds × k plus a margin (the engine draws actors up to about\n473\t     250 px), then `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)`; the patched engine draws the weapons.\n474\t   - `getImageData`, then for each pixel with alpha ≥ 128: depth = the view z of the actor's chest point − 4 u. If the\n475\t     depth test passes, write `color × actorLight` (mixed 60% toward white while the hit flash is on), and write the\n476\t     depth.\n477\t   - `actorLight = clamp(ambient + sun + Σ lights at the chest, 0.45, 1.25)`, tinted 25% toward the strongest light's\n478\t     color, so actors stay readable at night.\n479\t   - Draw a blob shadow before the actors: a dithered dark ellipse, 7 u in radius, on the ground under each.\n480\t7. **Splats and lines.**\n481\t   - Particles are projected points with a world-unit size. They are depth-tested, never write depth, and are either\n482\t     additive (sparks, fire, muzzle, glows) or dithered alpha (smoke, dust, spray).\n483\t   - Debris uses opaque pixels or `E.sprite` sprites.\n484\t   - Lines (tracers, laser sights, ropes, the lighthouse beam's edges) are depth-tested at their interpolated depth.\n485\t8. **Decals**: bullet holes and scorch marks on colliders' faces, with a depth bias of −0.5 u. A ring buffer holds 160.\n486\t9. **Canvas textures**:\n487\t   - signs, posters, and the festival's giant screens showing Krake's live broadcast, which is `rig.drawPortrait` into\n488\t     a 64×48 canvas, re-read every 4 frames;\n489\t   - consoles' screens;\n490\t   - text painted with `E.font`.\n491\t10. **Present**: `putImageData` into the buffer at the viewport's offset. Mirror `E.Mode7.draw`'s handling of\n492\t    `r.viewport`: `r.viewport || { x: 0, y: 0, w: r.bw, h: r.bh }`.\n493\t11. **Dev overlays** draw after `present`, in screen space: collider wireframes, hit volumes, cover points, rail paths,\n494\t    spawn markers, light radii, AI labels.\n495\t\n496\tCanvas work happens only in `draw`; headless, `draw` is skipped. Create scratch canvases lazily, the first time they\n497\tare drawn, so Node never needs a canvas.\n498\t\n499\t### 4.2 Camera, rail and cover\n500\t- **Logical pose**: the rail plus the slot plus the cover blend. It is deterministic and is the origin of every hitscan\n501\t  ray.\n502\t- **Presentation offsets**, visual only: trauma shake, weapon recoil, travel bob, carrier sway, hit jolt, breathing.\n503\t  They never move the aim ray. The reticle is drawn in screen space, so aiming stays exact.\n504\t- **Cover blend `c`** (0 out, 1 in):\n505\t  - it moves toward its target at `1/coverIn` (0.12 s) or `1/coverOut` (0.15 s);\n506\t  - **exposed means `c < 0.5`**;\n507\t  - pose: `pos = lerp(stand, cover, smooth(c))`, with yaw and pitch on the short way round; `roll = sin(πc)·2°`.\n508\t  - A cover pose sits 10–14 u lower and 6–16 u to the side, and is turned 8–20° away. The cover object fills 40–60% of\n509\t    the screen, so ducking reads instantly.\n510\t- **Arrival**:\n511\t  - each scene has an `arrive` path (`E.Path`, catmull) walked at its speed, easing in and out at the ends;\n512\t  - the look direction follows `look` keys along the path (`'path'` heading, or a world point), slerped;\n513\t  - at the end the camera blends into the slot's stand pose over 0.3 s, then \"ACTION!\" shows and TIME starts.\n514\t  - On foot (speed > 80 u/s): 2 Hz bob, 1.2 u.\n515\t- **Moving scenes**: slots ride a **carrier** (the boat, the funicular car, the tower lift) on its own path. The cover\n516\t  prop is part of the carrier, and waves trigger by the carrier's distance `d`. If enemies are still alive at the end\n517\t  of the path, the carrier stops until they're dead.\n518\t- **Shake**: trauma in 0..1, decaying 1.6/s; the offset is `noise(t·18) · trauma² · 2.5°`, scaled by `settings.shake`,\n519\t  and zero under reduce motion.\n520\t- **Recoil**: pitch kick per shot (handgun 0.6°, MG 0.25° each, shotgun 2.2°, GL 1.5°), with spring recovery.\n521\t- **Hit jolt**: roll 4°, pitch −2°, as a damped spring.\n522\t- **Carrier sway**: boats roll and pitch on slow noise of ±1.5°; the funicular swings ±1°.\n523\t- **Cutscene camera**: keys `[{ t, at, look, fov }]` with easing, plus 16-px letterbox bars. A cutscene can borrow the\n524\t  rail.\n525\t- **Free camera (dev)**:\n526\t  - fly with WASD or the left stick; look with the mouse or the right stick; Q/E or L1/R1 move down/up; Shift goes fast;\n527\t  - **Copy pose** writes `[x, y, z, yaw°, pitch°]` to the clipboard and the console for authoring.\n528\t\n529\t### 4.3 Controls, aiming and prompts\n530\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard + mouse | Keyboard only |\n531\t|---|---|---|---|\n532\t| Aim | Right stick (the left stick also aims) | Mouse (absolute reticle, like a light gun) | Arrows / WASD move the reticle |\n533\t| Fire | **R2** (Pad7), also ✕ (Pad0) | **Left click** | Z or J |\n534\t| Take cover (hold; toggle is a setting) | **L2** (Pad6), also ○ (Pad1) | **Right click** or **Space** | X or Shift |\n535\t| Next / previous weapon | R1 / L1 (Pad5/Pad4) | **Wheel**, E / Q | E / Q |\n536\t| Pick a weapon | — | 1 2 3 4 | 1 2 3 4 |\n537\t| Switch while hidden | Fire while in cover cycles to the next weapon with ammo | same | same |\n538\t| Quick reload (ducks for the reload time) | □ (Pad2) | R | R |\n539\t| Pause | Options (Pad9) | Esc / P | Esc / P |\n540\t| Cutscene: next line / skip (hold 0.6 s) | ✕ / hold ✕ | Click or Enter / hold Enter | Enter |\n541\t| Menus | D-pad or stick, ✕ confirm, ○ back | Hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls; Enter / Esc | Arrows, Enter, Esc |\n542\t\n543\tCreate (Pad8) is never bound: the AI Hub reserves Create+Options. In play, the d-pad nudges the pad reticle.\n544\t\n545\t**The aim model**, per player, in view units. The Sim receives only `aim` (exact, resolution-free).\n546\t- **Mouse.** `aim` comes from `input.pointer()`, mapped through that player's viewport rect, so it sits exactly under\n547\t  the pointer. Hide the OS cursor over the canvas while play is active and the last device is the mouse. When the\n548\t  pointer leaves the canvas, keep the last aim.\n549\t- **Pad.**\n550\t  - Response curve `v = sign(s)·|s|^2.2`; speed 1.6 vu/s × `padSpeed`.\n551\t  - Acceleration up to ×1.5 after the stick has been held 0.35 s.\n552\t  - **Aim assist** (Off, Low .35, Medium .6, High .85; Medium by default):\n553\t    - friction: speed × (1 − 0.55·assist) while the reticle is within 0.07 vu of a target's projected volume;\n554\t    - magnetism: `aim += (center − aim) · min(1, assist·6·dt)`, only while the stick moves (|s| > .2) and the target\n555\t      is within 0.12 vu;\n556\t    - targets are enemies, shootable projectiles, life rings and bottles; never civilians.\n557\t- **Keys.** A constant 1.2 vu/s.\n558\t- In cover the reticle hides. When you pop out, the pad keeps its position; the mouse follows the pointer.\n559\t- **Prompts** always come from `input.label(action)`: `game.input` for P1, `game.players[i]` for the others. They show\n560\t  in a rounded button frame and follow `input.lastDevice` (keyboard/mouse labels like \"Right click\"; the pad's glyph\n561\t  family from the Button symbols setting). Before any input, show both the pad and the keyboard/mouse prompts.\n562\t  Hardcoding a key name anywhere is a bug, and a test greps the source for it.\n563\t\n564\t### 4.4 Weapons\n565\t| | Handgun **\"P9 Marlin\"** | Machine gun **\"Riptide\"** | Shotgun **\"Breakwater\"** | Grenade launcher **\"Squall\"** |\n566\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n567\t| Ammo | 9-round magazine, unlimited reserve | pool, max 240, +60 a pickup | pool, max 36, +8 | pool, max 6, +2 |\n568\t| Fire | semi-auto, up to 6/s; holding auto-repeats at 3.5/s (Auto-fire setting) | full auto, 12/s held | pump, 1.5/s | 1/s |\n569\t| Damage | 1.0 | 0.45 | 8 pellets × 0.45 | 6 at the center, falling to 1.5 at 48 u |\n570\t| Spread | 0 | 0.6°, blooming to 3° (recovers 6°/s) | 5.5° cone | projectile, 640 u/s, gravity 240 u/s² |\n571\t| Special | reloads in cover in 0.30 s | shreds drones and crowds | staggers; knocks shields aside; knocks rushers back | wrecks vehicles, barricades, armor; splash hurts enemies only |\n572\t| Switch time | 0.20 s | 0.25 s | 0.30 s | 0.35 s |\n573\t\n574\t- Hits: head ×2 (HEADSHOT), torso ×1, legs ×0.75 plus a stagger.\n575\t- Special ammo carries over between scenes and stages within a run. An empty special switches back to the handgun.\n576\t- A weapon appears in the cycle once you have ammo for it.\n577\t- You can't fire while `c > 0.2`.\n578\t- \"RELOAD!\" flashes when the magazine is empty.\n579\t- Weapons are registry entries (`def('weapon', …)`) whose numbers are dev sliders.\n580\t\n581\t### 4.5 Player state, TIME and scoring\n582\t- **Lives:** Easy 7, Normal 5, Hard 3. Extra lives come from 150,000 and 400,000 points, and from the one life ring\n583\t  per stage.\n584\t- **After a hit:** 1.5 s of invulnerability, a red border pulse, the chain resets, a camera jolt, rumble (0.6, 0.25 s),\n585\t  and a voice grunt.\n586\t- **Out of lives:**\n587\t  - one player: the Sim freezes for **CONTINUE? 10…0**. Continuing restores lives, **resets the score to 0** (a\n588\t    continue mark is shown beside it) and restarts the current scene's waves.\n589\t  - co-op: each player counts down alone while the Sim runs.\n590\t  - AI Hub: an automatic return after 6 s with −5,000 points.\n591\t- **TIME:**\n592\t  - each scene has its seconds, × difficulty (Easy ×1.25, Hard ×0.85) × a dev slider. It counts only during ACTION\n593\t    and boss fights.\n594\t  - Killing an elite (Red Tide, a sniper) gives +2 s (\"TIME EXTEND +2\").\n595\t  - At 0: **\"TIDE'S IN!\"**, −1 life (this ignores invulnerability), and TIME refills to 60% (a slider).\n596\t  - On a clear: remaining seconds × 100 points.\n597\t\n598\t**Score**\n599\t\n600\t| Event | Points |\n601\t|---|---|\n602\t| Hit on an enemy | 100 |\n603\t| Kill | by archetype (§5.3) |\n604\t| Headshot | +500 |\n605\t| Quick kill (within 0.7 s of exposure) | +500 |\n606\t| A projectile shot down in the air | +1,000 |\n607\t| A breakable | 50 |\n608\t| A bonus target (bottle, ring) | 2,000 |\n609\t| A civilian or hostage hit | −5,000 and −1 life |\n610\t\n611\t**Chain.** +1 per hit. The multiplier is `1 + floor(chain/5)·0.1`, at most ×2.0. A miss (a shot that touches no enemy\n612\tand no bonus target) or a hit taken resets it.\n613\t\n614\t**Stage ranks**\n615\t\n616\t| Rank | Condition |\n617\t|---|---|\n618\t| S | accuracy ≥ 75% and no lives lost |\n619\t| A | accuracy ≥ 60% and ≤ 1 life lost |\n620\t| B | ≥ 45% |\n621\t| C | otherwise |\n622\t| D | a continue was used |\n623\t\n624\t**Stats:** shots, hits, accuracy, headshots, kills, quick kills, cover saves (threats blocked), lives lost, civilians\n625\thit, the best chain, time. They feed the results screen and the AI Hub stats.\n626\t\n627\t### 4.6 Enemies (framework; the roster is in §5.3)\n628\t**Cover points** in the scene data: `{ id, at: [x, y, z], face (yaw toward the players), h: 14 | 40, peek: ['up', 'left', 'right', 'over'], ledge: bool, water: bool }`.\n629\t\n630\t**The state machine**, with timings from the archetype and `sim.rng`:\n631\t```\n632\tSPAWN → ENTER(entry) → HIDE ⇄ PEEK → AIM (maybe a telegraph) → FIRE → HIDE …\n633\t                         ├→ RELOCATE (after n cycles, along a route to another cover point)\n634\t                         ├→ FLEE (quartermaster) / ADVANCE (rusher, shield)\n635\tany → HIT (react) → DEAD (death style) → CORPSE 2.5 s → dithered fade 0.5 s\n636\t```\n637\t**Entries**, each its own animation:\n638\t- `stand`: already there; pops up;\n639\t- `run`: along a route to the cover point;\n640\t- `jump`: down from a roof or container, with an arc, `air` and a heavy `land`;\n641\t- `rappel`: down a rope line, with `climb`, hand over hand;\n642\t- `door`: the door swings open, then the enemy runs out;\n643\t- `vault`: over a barrier;\n644\t- `roll`: a dive into cover (`flip`);\n645\t- `vehicle`: off a boat, jet ski or truck.\n646\t\n647\t**Peeks**:\n648\t- `up`: crouch → stand with `point: true` and `aim` toward the target camera;\n649\t- `left` / `right`: a 10-u side step with a 12° lean;\n650\t- `over`: a shield trooper fires over his shield.\n651\t\n652\tLeaning is done by turning the upper joints about the hips after `rig.update` (the Emberdeep technique: wrap\n653\t`solvePose`).\n654\t\n655\t**Fire plan.** On each exposure: aim for 0.35–0.6 s, then a burst of N shots. Each shot asks the ThreatDirector for\n656\t`threat` or `miss` (§4.7).\n657\t\n658\t**Hit volumes**, rebuilt from the rig's joints every step through `rig.worldOffset(J.*)`. `rig.update` runs headless,\n659\tso they are deterministic.\n660\t- head: a sphere of `headR·size·1.15` at `J.head`;\n661\t- torso: a capsule from the hip center to the neck, radius `torsoW·0.6·size`;\n662\t- legs: a capsule from the hips to the feet, radius 2.2·size;\n663\t- a shield: an oriented box in front of the left forearm, which blocks rays and sparks.\n664\t\n665\tA shot hits the nearest of (volumes ∪ colliders ∪ shootables ∪ civilians) along its ray.\n666\t\n667\t**Reactions**:\n668\t- a 0.06-s white flash; a flinch (`hurt` for 0.25 s);\n669\t- a stagger for leg hits or shotgun hits: knockback 30–60 u/s and a lean back;\n670\t- a headshot snaps the head back 25° (a joint post-process) and the body collapses.\n671\t\n672\t**Death styles**:\n673\t- `crumple`: the engine's `die` pose;\n674\t- `flyback`: a close shotgun blast or an explosion; velocity away from the blast, a roll (`flip`), then `down`;\n675\t- `fall`: off a ledge, with `air`, then lying down;\n676\t- `splash`: into the water, with spray, sinking;\n677\t- `spin`: an MG kill; a 120° turn into `die`.\n678\t\n679\t### 4.7 The ThreatDirector (the crisis flash)\n680\t- **The decision.** A shot becomes a threat if all of these hold:\n681\t  - `rng < chance × tune.threatMul × difficulty`;\n682\t  - the target has fewer active threats than `maxThreats` (Stage 1: 1, Stages 2–3: 2; Easy −1, Hard +1, minimum 1);\n683\t  - `minGap` (0.35 s) has passed since the target's last impact.\n684\t\n685\t  Otherwise it is a miss. Red Tide shots, snipers, rockets, rusher slashes, torpedoes and boss attacks are always\n686\t  threats, but they hold their aim longer to respect `minGap`. **No two impacts on one player land closer together\n687\t  than `minGap`.**\n688\t- **The telegraph**:\n689\t  - a ring at the muzzle's screen point, shrinking from 26 px to 6 px over the telegraph time, red with a white core\n690\t    and a \"!\" glyph (a \"Large\" setting doubles its size);\n691\t  - a muzzle flare and a dynamic light;\n692\t  - a tick at 66% and a sharp chirp at impact;\n693\t  - an edge arrow when the shooter is off screen;\n694\t  - threats aimed at teammates show only a small outline.\n695\t- **Pop-out protection.** If a player leaves cover with less than 0.15 s of a telegraph left, the impact slides 0.15 s\n696\t  later.\n697\t- **Resolution at impact time T**:\n698\t  - exposed, not invulnerable, not in god mode → **HIT** (§4.5);\n699\t  - otherwise **BLOCKED**: the bullet strikes the cover prop (sparks or splinters, a ricochet \"ping\") and the \"cover\n700\t    saves\" stat goes up.\n701\t  - Online, the host resolves at T + 0.2 s, using the cover timeline the client reported (§4.19).\n702\t- **Misses**:\n703\t  - a tracer from the muzzle to a point 8–30 u off the camera, never through the reticle;\n704\t  - a whizz panned by screen x;\n705\t  - an impact effect on the nearest collider behind: metal sparks, wood splinters, concrete dust, water spray, glass.\n706\t\n707\t### 4.8 Projectiles, objects, pickups, civilians and allies\n708\t**Projectiles**: `{ kind, pos, vel, gravity, hp, r (shootable radius), team, target }`. Every one is shootable and\n709\tshows a shrinking ring while it is a threat.\n710\t- **Enemy grenade**: lobbed to land 30–60 u in front of the target's cover in 1.1–1.4 s; hp 1. It is a threat at\n711\t  landing. Shot down in the air, it explodes there, hurting enemies within 48 u, for +1,000.\n712\t- **Rocket**: 220 u/s with a smoke trail; hp 1; a threat on arrival; shot down for +1,000.\n713\t- **Harpoon** (Barracuda): 300 u/s with a rope line; hp 2.\n714\t- **Torpedo**: runs on the surface at 120 u/s with a wake; hp 2. **Cover does not stop it: you have to shoot it.** A\n715\t  big prompt says \"SHOOT THE TORPEDO!\".\n716\t- **Missile** (Scylla): an arc; hp 1.\n717\t- **Flare** (Krake): 160 u/s and glowing; hp 1; it bursts into firework sparks.\n718\t- **Player GL round**: explodes on contact with a collider, an actor or water, or after 1.2 s.\n719\t\n720\t**Breakables** `{ hp, debris, onBreak }`:\n721\t\n722\t| Breakable | hp | Notes |\n723\t|---|---|---|\n724\t| Wooden crate | 3 | Breaking it removes its collider, which can expose an enemy |\n725\t| Window | 1 | Glass |\n726\t| Paper lantern | 1 | Falls and burns for 2 s |\n727\t| Bottles and market goods | 1 | Cosmetic, 50 points |\n728\t| Sign | 2 | Swings |\n729\t| Gallery and barricade sections | scripted | |\n730\t\n731\t**Explosive barrels and tanks**: hp 2, radius 56 u, damage 8. Neighbors chain-explode after 0.15 s. They leave scorch\n732\tdecals, a light, shake and debris.\n733\t\n734\t**Pickups**:\n735\t- Quartermaster ammo, granted on the kill with a banner and the icon flying to the weapon panel;\n736\t- the **life ring** (red and white; shoot it): +1 life, \"1UP!\";\n737\t- the **intel bottle** (green glass; shoot it): added to the INTEL list (§4.20).\n738\t\n739\t**Civilians** (festival-goers, dock workers, fishermen in yellow raincoats) and **hostages**:\n740\t- they have hit volumes and stop bullets; hitting one costs −1 life (even through invulnerability) and −5,000;\n741\t- radio: \"Hold your fire!\";\n742\t- civilians run routes and crouch.\n743\t\n744\t**Allies** (single player): Rook and Mari as scripted actors.\n745\t- Shots pass through them, with a \"Hey—watch it!\" bark at most every 5 s.\n746\t- At cues they make scripted kills, shown as a tracer from the ally.\n747\t- In co-op they appear only in cutscenes.\n748\t\n749\t### 4.9 Bosses (framework)\n750\t```js\n751\tdef('boss', 'stingray', { name: 'STINGRAY', title: 'Black Current gunboat', time: 100,\n752\t  body: G => buildStingrayRig(G),                 // a mesh rig: hull and turret parts, posed by code\n753\t  parts: { turretL: { hp: 12, vol: [...], weak: true }, … },\n754\t  bars: [{ label: 'STINGRAY', parts: ['turretL', 'turretR'] }, { label: 'BARRACUDA', actor: 'barracuda' }],\n755\t  script: function* (B, sim) { /* phases: yields of seconds, predicates, sub-patterns */ } });\n756\t```\n757\t- **Parts** are named hit volumes on a mesh rig's transforms or on a Humanoid's joints. Each has hp and optional armor.\n758\t  A `weak` part pulses gold while it can be damaged, and breaking a part plays its own effects.\n759\t- **HP bars** sit under TIME, with a label and a segment per part. They flash on damage.\n760\t- **Intro**: a 2.5-s name card (\"BARRACUDA — Captain of the Black Current\"), a close-up, a music sting. In AI Hub\n761\t  sessions only the card shows; the camera isn't taken away.\n762\t- Every attack goes through the ThreatDirector and the projectile system, so cover, telegraphs and the tests all work\n763\t  the same for bosses.\n764\t- **Defeat**: 0.6 s of slow motion (`timeScale` 0.35, presentation-side), chained explosions, the death animation,\n765\t  then the outro.\n766\t\n767\t### 4.10 Stage format, the Director and the validators\n768\t```js\n769\tdef('stage', 1, { name: 'HARBOR LIGHTS', place: 'Port Lumen Docks', clock: '23:40', music: 'harbor',\n770\t  env: { sky: 'festival', fog: ['#1a2242', 400, 2000], ambient: .38,\n771\t         sun: { dir: [-.35, .45, .82], color: '#a8b8ff', k: .45 }, water: { z: -24, mat: 'sea-night' },\n772\t         weather: null, fireworks: true },\n773\t  build(G) { /* geometry: brushes and prefabs (§5.10) */ },\n774\t  intro: 'c1-intro', outro: 'c1-outro', scenes: ['1-1', '1-2', '1-3', '1-4', '1-B'] });\n775\t\n776\tdef('scene', '1-1', { title: 'LANTERN PIER', time: 45,\n777\t  arrive: { path: [[-160, 0, 24], [-60, -10, 24], [0, -20, 24]], speed: 110,\n778\t            look: [[0, 'path'], [.8, [300, 0, 18]]] },\n779\t  slots: [   // P1 on the left, P2 on the right (facing east, +y is to the right)\n780\t    { stand: { at: [0, -20, 24], look: [300, -10, 16] }, cover: { at: [-6, -32, 12], look: [300, -60, 10] } },\n781\t    { stand: { at: [0, 22, 24], look: [300, 10, 16] },   cover: { at: [-6, 34, 12], look: [300, 60, 10] } } ],\n782\t  covers: { c1: { at: [160, -30, 0], h: 16, peek: ['up'] },\n783\t            c2: { at: [180, 30, 0], h: 16, peek: ['up', 'right'] }, … },\n784\t  waves: [\n785\t    { at: .5, spawn: [['deckhand', 'c1', { entry: 'stand' }], ['deckhand', 'c2'], ['deckhand', 'c3']] },\n786\t    { after: 'clear', spawn: [['deckhand', 'c5', { entry: 'door' }], ['deckhand', 'c4', { entry: 'jump' }]] },\n787\t    { after: 'clear', spawn: [['quartermaster', null, { route: [[360, -80, 0], [360, 90, 0]], drop: 'mg', flee: 3.5 }],\n788\t                              ['deckhand', 'c6', { entry: 'run', from: [420, -60, 0] }]] } ],\n789\t  civilians: [{ at: 2, kind: 'festival-pair', route: [[120, -50, 0], [300, -50, 0]] }],\n790\t  objects: [['lifering', [100, 58, 18]], ['lanterns', 'stalls'], ['bottles', 'c1']],\n791\t  cues: [{ at: 0, say: ['juno', 'Hostiles on the pier! Watch the festival crowd!'] },\n792\t         { on: 'spawn:quartermaster', say: ['juno', \"Orange vest! He's hauling ammo—drop him!\"] }] });\n793\t```\n794\t- **Director phases**: `intro` (cutscene) → `travel` (WAIT) → `action` → `clear` (the time bonus counts up, 1.2 s)\n795\t  → `travel` … → `boss` → `outro` → results.\n796\t- A wave starts by `at` (seconds into the action), `atD` (carrier distance), `after: 'clear' | '<waveId>' | 'kills:N'`,\n797\t  or `on: '<event>'`.\n798\t- A scene clears when every non-civilian it spawned is dead and no wave is pending.\n799\t- Cues: `say` (radio, not modal), `cut` (a short modal line), `prompt` (a tutorial hint, the first time only), `fx`\n800\t  (an explosion, a door, a vehicle), `music` (a layer or a sting).\n801\t- **AI Hub slots 3–4.** Scenes author two slots. Slot 3 = slot 1 moved 28 u further left, perpendicular to the view;\n802\t  slot 4 = slot 2 moved 28 u further right. Each gets an auto cover prop (a crate pair 20 u ahead of its cover pose)\n803\t  unless the scene authors one. The validators check them like real slots.\n804\t\n805\t**Validators** (tests run them over every stage):\n806\t- every id resolves (types, covers, speakers, songs, cutscenes);\n807\t- every cover point's **exposed** head and chest are inside the view (|ax| < 0.95A, |ay| < 0.95) from every slot's\n808\t  stand pose, with a clear ray (no collider in the way). This means every enemy can be shot;\n809\t- every **hidden** pose is fully occluded from every slot (the cover really works);\n810\t- no camera point, sampled along every arrive path and every slot pose, is inside a solid or within 4 u of a wall in\n811\t  front of it;\n812\t- paths are continuous; carriers' slots stay on their carrier;\n813\t- each scene's time allowance is at least 1.3 × the fair bot's clear time (measured by `--playthrough`; the result is\n814\t  stored in `tests/balance.json`);\n815\t- the scene lists in the attract playlist, the dev jump list and the stage lists match.\n816\t\n817\t### 4.11 Story, dialogue and cutscenes\n818\t- **Speakers**: `def('speaker', id, { name, color, rig: { …Humanoid options }, voice: '<voice id>' })`. The portraits\n819\t  are live: `rig.drawPortrait` inside `E.Dialog`.\n820\t- **Cutscene pages** are modal: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig, onDone })` with the engine's markup:\n821\t  `{c=#ffd36a}…{/c}` for color, `{w=.4}` for a pause, `{shake}…{/shake}` for shouting.\n822\t- **Radio** is not modal: `{ auto: 2.5–4, modal: false, place: 'top' }`, one line at a time and queued. A Subtitles\n823\t  setting hides the text but keeps the voices.\n824\t- **Voice sync.** Set `talk.voice = null`. When a page starts, `Voice.say(text, speakerVoice)` plays it, and the\n825\t  Dialog's typing speed matches the voice's syllable rate.\n826\t- **Cutscene scripts** are generators on the Sim's runner. Steps: `cam(keys)`, `actor(id, { at, path, pose, expr })`,\n827\t  `say(id, text)`, `radio(id, text)`, `wait(s)`, `fx(…)`, `music(…)`.\n828\t  - Press confirm to advance a line; **hold confirm for 0.6 s to skip** (\"HOLD ✕ TO SKIP\" in the corner).\n829\t  - Skipping jumps to the cutscene's end state.\n830\t  - In AI Hub sessions, cutscenes are skipped; the radio and the boss cards stay.\n831\t- **Briefings** (one per stage) are pages over a stylized map of the Lumen Isles drawn with `px`, with Director Hale's\n832\t  and Juno's portraits and two or three objectives. They are skippable.\n833\t\n834\t### 4.12 Audio: music, SFX and voices (everything written in code)\n835\t- **Music** uses the engine tracker `{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes, name, on }] }` (§5.12).\n836\t  - Tracks are named so `audio.layer` can bring the drums and the lead in during ACTION and drop them during WAIT.\n837\t  - `audio.tempo(+8 bpm)` while TIME < 10 s.\n838\t  - Stings (stage clear, boss intro, extra life) via `audio.sting`.\n839\t  - Crossfade between scenes with `music(song, { fade: 1 })`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14efaf47-c831-41be-b2ba-0398106a097f","uuid":"165fcf28-20c1-498a-94bd-fb82bcfc00f3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:32:27.180Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183213__xf5fb84/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 4.1 Rail3D, the renderer\n**Data**\n- **Mesh**: `{ groups: [{ mat, pos: Float32Array, nrm, uv, lit: Float32Array (baked light per vertex), flags }], bounds, sphere }`.\n- **TextureBank** (built at boot):\n  - each material has 5 band tiles: `E.materialTile(id, { size: 32, px: 2, band: 0..4 })`, plus `lid: true` versions\n    for faces that look up or down. Each tile is converted to a `Uint32Array`.\n  - Tiles are `v`-up with the top row first, so flip v when sampling.\n  - Face-pattern materials (crate) use uv 0..1 across each face.\n  - Use **named** materials in shape entries so `materialTile(id)` resolves them. Register any inline material in\n    `E.MATERIALS` under a generated id first.\n  - Material flags: `glow` (emissive), `water`, `double` (draw both sides), `alphaTest` (nets, grates, foliage cards,\n    on the tile's alpha or a pattern mask), `scroll` (uv speed).\n- **Instances**: `{ mesh, M (3×4), visible, dynamic, tint, flash, collider ids }`.\n- **Static world**: built when a stage is built, then transformed to world space and baked. The baked light is\n  `ambient + sunK·max(0, n·sun) + Σ static lights` plus contact darkening where a wall meets the floor. Tessellate\n  faces to edges of 64 u or less so the vertex light, fog and near clipping stay clean. Bin it into 256-u chunks with\n  bounding spheres for frustum culling.\n\n**Per viewport, per frame**\n1. `begin(cam, rect)`: the basis, `F`, the frustum planes; clear the depth to `+∞`.\n2. **Sky**, per pixel from the ray's elevation e and azimuth a:\n   - a three-stop gradient (the stage palette);\n   - stars from a hash of the quantized direction where e > 0.05, twinkling;\n   - a moon or sun disc with a halo;\n   - two octaves of drifting noise clouds;\n   - one to three distant silhouette layers on the horizon (`h(a)` heightfields: skyline, cliffs, the barrier);\n   - lightning brightens it.\n3. **Triangles**, for each visible group:\n   - transform to view space, clip against the near plane (z = 2 u; Sutherland–Hodgman, a triangle becomes 0–2),\n     project, and cull back faces unless the material is `double`;\n   - rasterize over the integer bounding box with edge functions and a top-left fill rule. Interpolate `1/z`, `u/z`,\n     `v/z` and `L/z` for perspective-correct values;\n   - depth test on view z;\n   - per pixel: `band = clamp(floor(L·4 + bayer4[x&3][y&3]), 0, 4)` (the engine's banded light, dithered between\n     bands) → texel → fog → write color and depth;\n   - fog `f = clamp((z − near)/(far − near))^1.2`, quantized to 8 steps with the Bayer threshold. Emissive materials\n     skip lighting and take half the fog.\n   - *Retro wobble* (a setting, off by default) snaps vertices to whole pixels and makes texturing affine.\n4. **Dynamic light** on top of the baked light: up to 12 point lights per frame (muzzle flashes, explosions, flares,\n   the lighthouse beam, the tram's lamps). Each adds `k·(1 − d/r)²` per vertex, only for triangles whose bounding\n   sphere it touches.\n5. **Water**: a material with `water: true`.\n   - The band comes from `base + 0.6·sin(u·.11 + t·1.3)·sin(v·.07 − t)` plus noise.\n   - Sparkles: the `hi` color where noise(u + t·8, v) > 0.82, weighted toward the moon or sun direction.\n   - Vertex foam near shores and sluices.\n   - Anything that falls below the water height spawns a splash.\n6. **Actors as billboards.** Sort far to near; for each actor:\n   - project the foot point; `k = F/z`; skip it if it is under 3 px tall or off screen.\n   - Choose the view with `new E.View('r3', 'Rail3D', yawDeg, pitchDeg, k, 1)`. Take `yawDeg` from the\n     **camera→actor bearing** `b`: `−b·180/π − 90`, as `E.Mode7.view` does from the heading. Take `pitchDeg` from the\n     ray's downward elevation, **clamped to at least 3°** (a View at 1° or less becomes a side view, and Views can't\n     look up). Cache the views by quantized key: yaw and pitch in 2° steps, k in 1/16 steps.\n   - Clear a scratch canvas sized to the actor's bounds × k plus a margin (the engine draws actors up to about\n     250 px), then `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)`; the patched engine draws the weapons.\n   - `getImageData`, then for each pixel with alpha ≥ 128: depth = the view z of the actor's chest point − 4 u. If the\n     depth test passes, write `color × actorLight` (mixed 60% toward white while the hit flash is on), and write the\n     depth.\n   - `actorLight = clamp(ambient + sun + Σ lights at the chest, 0.45, 1.25)`, tinted 25% toward the strongest light's\n     color, so actors stay readable at night.\n   - Draw a blob shadow before the actors: a dithered dark ellipse, 7 u in radius, on the ground under each.\n7. **Splats and lines.**\n   - Particles are projected points with a world-unit size. They are depth-tested, never write depth, and are either\n     additive (sparks, fire, muzzle, glows) or dithered alpha (smoke, dust, spray).\n   - Debris uses opaque pixels or `E.sprite` sprites.\n   - Lines (tracers, laser sights, ropes, the lighthouse beam's edges) are depth-tested at their interpolated depth.\n8. **Decals**: bullet holes and scorch marks on colliders' faces, with a depth bias of −0.5 u. A ring buffer holds 160.\n9. **Canvas textures**:\n   - signs, posters, and the festival's giant screens showing Krake's live broadcast, which is `rig.drawPortrait` into\n     a 64×48 canvas, re-read every 4 frames;\n   - consoles' screens;\n   - text painted with `E.font`.\n10. **Present**: `putImageData` into the buffer at the viewport's offset. Mirror `E.Mode7.draw`'s handling of\n    `r.viewport`: `r.viewport || { x: 0, y: 0, w: r.bw, h: r.bh }`.\n11. **Dev overlays** draw after `present`, in screen space: collider wireframes, hit volumes, cover points, rail paths,\n    spawn markers, light radii, AI labels.\n\nCanvas work happens only in `draw`; headless, `draw` is skipped. Create scratch canvases lazily, the first time they\nare drawn, so Node never needs a canvas.\n\n### 4.2 Camera, rail and cover\n- **Logical pose**: the rail plus the slot plus the cover blend. It is deterministic and is the origin of every hitscan\n  ray.\n- **Presentation offsets**, visual only: trauma shake, weapon recoil, travel bob, carrier sway, hit jolt, breathing.\n  They never move the aim ray. The reticle is drawn in screen space, so aiming stays exact.\n- **Cover blend `c`** (0 out, 1 in):\n  - it moves toward its target at `1/coverIn` (0.12 s) or `1/coverOut` (0.15 s);\n  - **exposed means `c < 0.5`**;\n  - pose: `pos = lerp(stand, cover, smooth(c))`, with yaw and pitch on the short way round; `roll = sin(πc)·2°`.\n  - A cover pose sits 10–14 u lower and 6–16 u to the side, and is turned 8–20° away. The cover object fills 40–60% of\n    the screen, so ducking reads instantly.\n- **Arrival**:\n  - each scene has an `arrive` path (`E.Path`, catmull) walked at its speed, easing in and out at the ends;\n  - the look direction follows `look` keys along the path (`'path'` heading, or a world point), slerped;\n  - at the end the camera blends into the slot's stand pose over 0.3 s, then \"ACTION!\" shows and TIME starts.\n  - On foot (speed > 80 u/s): 2 Hz bob, 1.2 u.\n- **Moving scenes**: slots ride a **carrier** (the boat, the funicular car, the tower lift) on its own path. The cover\n  prop is part of the carrier, and waves trigger by the carrier's distance `d`. If enemies are still alive at the end\n  of the path, the carrier stops until they're dead.\n- **Shake**: trauma in 0..1, decaying 1.6/s; the offset is `noise(t·18) · trauma² · 2.5°`, scaled by `settings.shake`,\n  and zero under reduce motion.\n- **Recoil**: pitch kick per shot (handgun 0.6°, MG 0.25° each, shotgun 2.2°, GL 1.5°), with spring recovery.\n- **Hit jolt**: roll 4°, pitch −2°, as a damped spring.\n- **Carrier sway**: boats roll and pitch on slow noise of ±1.5°; the funicular swings ±1°.\n- **Cutscene camera**: keys `[{ t, at, look, fov }]` with easing, plus 16-px letterbox bars. A cutscene can borrow the\n  rail.\n- **Free camera (dev)**:\n  - fly with WASD or the left stick; look with the mouse or the right stick; Q/E or L1/R1 move down/up; Shift goes fast;\n  - **Copy pose** writes `[x, y, z, yaw°, pitch°]` to the clipboard and the console for authoring.\n\n### 4.3 Controls, aiming and prompts\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Keyboard + mouse | Keyboard only |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Aim | Right stick (the left stick also aims) | Mouse (absolute reticle, like a light gun) | Arrows / WASD move the reticle |\n| Fire | **R2** (Pad7), also ✕ (Pad0) | **Left click** | Z or J |\n| Take cover (hold; toggle is a setting) | **L2** (Pad6), also ○ (Pad1) | **Right click** or **Space** | X or Shift |\n| Next / previous weapon | R1 / L1 (Pad5/Pad4) | **Wheel**, E / Q | E / Q |\n| Pick a weapon | — | 1 2 3 4 | 1 2 3 4 |\n| Switch while hidden | Fire while in cover cycles to the next weapon with ammo | same | same |\n| Quick reload (ducks for the reload time) | □ (Pad2) | R | R |\n| Pause | Options (Pad9) | Esc / P | Esc / P |\n| Cutscene: next line / skip (hold 0.6 s) | ✕ / hold ✕ | Click or Enter / hold Enter | Enter |\n| Menus | D-pad or stick, ✕ confirm, ○ back | Hover focuses, click picks, wheel scrolls; Enter / Esc | Arrows, Enter, Esc |\n\nCreate (Pad8) is never bound: the AI Hub reserves Create+Options. In play, the d-pad nudges the pad reticle.\n\n**The aim model**, per player, in view units. The Sim receives only `aim` (exact, resolution-free).\n- **Mouse.** `aim` comes from `input.pointer()`, mapped through that player's viewport rect, so it sits exactly under\n  the pointer. Hide the OS cursor over the canvas while play is active and the last device is the mouse. When the\n  pointer leaves the canvas, keep the last aim.\n- **Pad.**\n  - Response curve `v = sign(s)·|s|^2.2`; speed 1.6 vu/s × `padSpeed`.\n  - Acceleration up to ×1.5 after the stick has been held 0.35 s.\n  - **Aim assist** (Off, Low .35, Medium .6, High .85; Medium by default):\n    - friction: speed × (1 − 0.55·assist) while the reticle is within 0.07 vu of a target's projected volume;\n    - magnetism: `aim += (center − aim) · min(1, assist·6·dt)`, only while the stick moves (|s| > .2) and the target\n      is within 0.12 vu;\n    - targets are enemies, shootable projectiles, life rings and bottles; never civilians.\n- **Keys.** A constant 1.2 vu/s.\n- In cover the reticle hides. When you pop out, the pad keeps its position; the mouse follows the pointer.\n- **Prompts** always come from `input.label(action)`: `game.input` for P1, `game.players[i]` for the others. They show\n  in a rounded button frame and follow `input.lastDevice` (keyboard/mouse labels like \"Right click\"; the pad's glyph\n  family from the Button symbols setting). Before any input, show both the pad and the keyboard/mouse prompts.\n  Hardcoding a key name anywhere is a bug, and a test greps the source for it.\n\n### 4.4 Weapons\n| | Handgun **\"P9 Marlin\"** | Machine gun **\"Riptide\"** | Shotgun **\"Breakwater\"** | Grenade launcher **\"Squall\"** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Ammo | 9-round magazine, unlimited reserve | pool, max 240, +60 a pickup | pool, max 36, +8 | pool, max 6, +2 |\n| Fire | semi-auto, up to 6/s; holding auto-repeats at 3.5/s (Auto-fire setting) | full auto, 12/s held | pump, 1.5/s | 1/s |\n| Damage | 1.0 | 0.45 | 8 pellets × 0.45 | 6 at the center, falling to 1.5 at 48 u |\n| Spread | 0 | 0.6°, blooming to 3° (recovers 6°/s) | 5.5° cone | projectile, 640 u/s, gravity 240 u/s² |\n| Special | reloads in cover in 0.30 s | shreds drones and crowds | staggers; knocks shields aside; knocks rushers back | wrecks vehicles, barricades, armor; splash hurts enemies only |\n| Switch time | 0.20 s | 0.25 s | 0.30 s | 0.35 s |\n\n- Hits: head ×2 (HEADSHOT), torso ×1, legs ×0.75 plus a stagger.\n- Special ammo carries over between scenes and stages within a run. An empty special switches back to the handgun.\n- A weapon appears in the cycle once you have ammo for it.\n- You can't fire while `c > 0.2`.\n- \"RELOAD!\" flashes when the magazine is empty.\n- Weapons are registry entries (`def('weapon', …)`) whose numbers are dev sliders.\n\n### 4.5 Player state, TIME and scoring\n- **Lives:** Easy 7, Normal 5, Hard 3. Extra lives come from 150,000 and 400,000 points, and from the one life ring\n  per stage.\n- **After a hit:** 1.5 s of invulnerability, a red border pulse, the chain resets, a camera jolt, rumble (0.6, 0.25 s),\n  and a voice grunt.\n- **Out of lives:**\n  - one player: the Sim freezes for **CONTINUE? 10…0**. Continuing restores lives, **resets the score to 0** (a\n    continue mark is shown beside it) and restarts the current scene's waves.\n  - co-op: each player counts down alone while the Sim runs.\n  - AI Hub: an automatic return after 6 s with −5,000 points.\n- **TIME:**\n  - each scene has its seconds, × difficulty (Easy ×1.25, Hard ×0.85) × a dev slider. It counts only during ACTION\n    and boss fights.\n  - Killing an elite (Red Tide, a sniper) gives +2 s (\"TIME EXTEND +2\").\n  - At 0: **\"TIDE'S IN!\"**, −1 life (this ignores invulnerability), and TIME refills to 60% (a slider).\n  - On a clear: remaining seconds × 100 points.\n\n**Score**\n\n| Event | Points |\n|---|---|\n| Hit on an enemy | 100 |\n| Kill | by archetype (§5.3) |\n| Headshot | +500 |\n| Quick kill (within 0.7 s of exposure) | +500 |\n| A projectile shot down in the air | +1,000 |\n| A breakable | 50 |\n| A bonus target (bottle, ring) | 2,000 |\n| A civilian or hostage hit | −5,000 and −1 life |\n\n**Chain.** +1 per hit. The multiplier is `1 + floor(chain/5)·0.1`, at most ×2.0. A miss (a shot that touches no enemy\nand no bonus target) or a hit taken resets it.\n\n**Stage ranks**\n\n| Rank | Condition |\n|---|---|\n| S | accuracy ≥ 75% and no lives lost |\n| A | accuracy ≥ 60% and ≤ 1 life lost |\n| B | ≥ 45% |\n| C | otherwise |\n| D | a continue was used |\n\n**Stats:** shots, hits, accuracy, headshots, kills, quick kills, cover saves (threats blocked), lives lost, civilians\nhit, the best chain, time. They feed the results screen and the AI Hub stats.\n\n### 4.6 Enemies (framework; the roster is in §5.3)\n**Cover points** in the scene data: `{ id, at: [x, y, z], face (yaw toward the players), h: 14 | 40, peek: ['up', 'left', 'right', 'over'], ledge: bool, water: bool }`.\n\n**The state machine**, with timings from the archetype and `sim.rng`:\n```\nSPAWN → ENTER(entry) → HIDE ⇄ PEEK → AIM (maybe a telegraph) → FIRE → HIDE …\n                         ├→ RELOCATE (after n cycles, along a route to another cover point)\n                         ├→ FLEE (quartermaster) / ADVANCE (rusher, shield)\nany → HIT (react) → DEAD (death style) → CORPSE 2.5 s → dithered fade 0.5 s\n```\n**Entries**, each its own animation:\n- `stand`: already there; pops up;\n- `run`: along a route to the cover point;\n- `jump`: down from a roof or container, with an arc, `air` and a heavy `land`;\n- `rappel`: down a rope line, with `climb`, hand over hand;\n- `door`: the door swings open, then the enemy runs out;\n- `vault`: over a barrier;\n- `roll`: a dive into cover (`flip`);\n- `vehicle`: off a boat, jet ski or truck.\n\n**Peeks**:\n- `up`: crouch → stand with `point: true` and `aim` toward the target camera;\n- `left` / `right`: a 10-u side step with a 12° lean;\n- `over`: a shield trooper fires over his shield.\n\nLeaning is done by turning the upper joints about the hips after `rig.update` (the Emberdeep technique: wrap\n`solvePose`).\n\n**Fire plan.** On each exposure: aim for 0.35–0.6 s, then a burst of N shots. Each shot asks the ThreatDirector for\n`threat` or `miss` (§4.7).\n\n**Hit volumes**, rebuilt from the rig's joints every step through `rig.worldOffset(J.*)`. `rig.update` runs headless,\nso they are deterministic.\n- head: a sphere of `headR·size·1.15` at `J.head`;\n- torso: a capsule from the hip center to the neck, radius `torsoW·0.6·size`;\n- legs: a capsule from the hips to the feet, radius 2.2·size;\n- a shield: an oriented box in front of the left forearm, which blocks rays and sparks.\n\nA shot hits the nearest of (volumes ∪ colliders ∪ shootables ∪ civilians) along its ray.\n\n**Reactions**:\n- a 0.06-s white flash; a flinch (`hurt` for 0.25 s);\n- a stagger for leg hits or shotgun hits: knockback 30–60 u/s and a lean back;\n- a headshot snaps the head back 25° (a joint post-process) and the body collapses.\n\n**Death styles**:\n- `crumple`: the engine's `die` pose;\n- `flyback`: a close shotgun blast or an explosion; velocity away from the blast, a roll (`flip`), then `down`;\n- `fall`: off a ledge, with `air`, then lying down;\n- `splash`: into the water, with spray, sinking;\n- `spin`: an MG kill; a 120° turn into `die`.\n\n### 4.7 The ThreatDirector (the crisis flash)\n- **The decision.** A shot becomes a threat if all of these hold:\n  - `rng < chance × tune.threatMul × difficulty`;\n  - the target has fewer active threats than `maxThreats` (Stage 1: 1, Stages 2–3: 2; Easy −1, Hard +1, minimum 1);\n  - `minGap` (0.35 s) has passed since the target's last impact.\n\n  Otherwise it is a miss. Red Tide shots, snipers, rockets, rusher slashes, torpedoes and boss attacks are always\n  threats, but they hold their aim longer to respect `minGap`. **No two impacts on one player land closer together\n  than `minGap`.**\n- **The telegraph**:\n  - a ring at the muzzle's screen point, shrinking from 26 px to 6 px over the telegraph time, red with a white core\n    and a \"!\" glyph (a \"Large\" setting doubles its size);\n  - a muzzle flare and a dynamic light;\n  - a tick at 66% and a sharp chirp at impact;\n  - an edge arrow when the shooter is off screen;\n  - threats aimed at teammates show only a small outline.\n- **Pop-out protection.** If a player leaves cover with less than 0.15 s of a telegraph left, the impact slides 0.15 s\n  later.\n- **Resolution at impact time T**:\n  - exposed, not invulnerable, not in god mode → **HIT** (§4.5);\n  - otherwise **BLOCKED**: the bullet strikes the cover prop (sparks or splinters, a ricochet \"ping\") and the \"cover\n    saves\" stat goes up.\n  - Online, the host resolves at T + 0.2 s, using the cover timeline the client reported (§4.19).\n- **Misses**:\n  - a tracer from the muzzle to a point 8–30 u off the camera, never through the reticle;\n  - a whizz panned by screen x;\n  - an impact effect on the nearest collider behind: metal sparks, wood splinters, concrete dust, water spray, glass.\n\n### 4.8 Projectiles, objects, pickups, civilians and allies\n**Projectiles**: `{ kind, pos, vel, gravity, hp, r (shootable radius), team, target }`. Every one is shootable and\nshows a shrinking ring while it is a threat.\n- **Enemy grenade**: lobbed to land 30–60 u in front of the target's cover in 1.1–1.4 s; hp 1. It is a threat at\n  landing. Shot down in the air, it explodes there, hurting enemies within 48 u, for +1,000.\n- **Rocket**: 220 u/s with a smoke trail; hp 1; a threat on arrival; shot down for +1,000.\n- **Harpoon** (Barracuda): 300 u/s with a rope line; hp 2.\n- **Torpedo**: runs on the surface at 120 u/s with a wake; hp 2. **Cover does not stop it: you have to shoot it.** A\n  big prompt says \"SHOOT THE TORPEDO!\".\n- **Missile** (Scylla): an arc; hp 1.\n- **Flare** (Krake): 160 u/s and glowing; hp 1; it bursts into firework sparks.\n- **Player GL round**: explodes on contact with a collider, an actor or water, or after 1.2 s.\n\n**Breakables** `{ hp, debris, onBreak }`:\n\n| Breakable | hp | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wooden crate | 3 | Breaking it removes its collider, which can expose an enemy |\n| Window | 1 | Glass |\n| Paper lantern | 1 | Falls and burns for 2 s |\n| Bottles and market goods | 1 | Cosmetic, 50 points |\n| Sign | 2 | Swings |\n| Gallery and barricade sections | scripted | |\n\n**Explosive barrels and tanks**: hp 2, radius 56 u, damage 8. Neighbors chain-explode after 0.15 s. They leave scorch\ndecals, a light, shake and debris.\n\n**Pickups**:\n- Quartermaster ammo, granted on the kill with a banner and the icon flying to the weapon panel;\n- the **life ring** (red and white; shoot it): +1 life, \"1UP!\";\n- the **intel bottle** (green glass; shoot it): added to the INTEL list (§4.20).\n\n**Civilians** (festival-goers, dock workers, fishermen in yellow raincoats) and **hostages**:\n- they have hit volumes and stop bullets; hitting one costs −1 life (even through invulnerability) and −5,000;\n- radio: \"Hold your fire!\";\n- civilians run routes and crouch.\n\n**Allies** (single player): Rook and Mari as scripted actors.\n- Shots pass through them, with a \"Hey—watch it!\" bark at most every 5 s.\n- At cues they make scripted kills, shown as a tracer from the ally.\n- In co-op they appear only in cutscenes.\n\n### 4.9 Bosses (framework)\n```js\ndef('boss', 'stingray', { name: 'STINGRAY', title: 'Black Current gunboat', time: 100,\n  body: G => buildStingrayRig(G),                 // a mesh rig: hull and turret parts, posed by code\n  parts: { turretL: { hp: 12, vol: [...], weak: true }, … },\n  bars: [{ label: 'STINGRAY', parts: ['turretL', 'turretR'] }, { label: 'BARRACUDA', actor: 'barracuda' }],\n  script: function* (B, sim) { /* phases: yields of seconds, predicates, sub-patterns */ } });\n```\n- **Parts** are named hit volumes on a mesh rig's transforms or on a Humanoid's joints. Each has hp and optional armor.\n  A `weak` part pulses gold while it can be damaged, and breaking a part plays its own effects.\n- **HP bars** sit under TIME, with a label and a segment per part. They flash on damage.\n- **Intro**: a 2.5-s name card (\"BARRACUDA — Captain of the Black Current\"), a close-up, a music sting. In AI Hub\n  sessions only the card shows; the camera isn't taken away.\n- Every attack goes through the ThreatDirector and the projectile system, so cover, telegraphs and the tests all work\n  the same for bosses.\n- **Defeat**: 0.6 s of slow motion (`timeScale` 0.35, presentation-side), chained explosions, the death animation,\n  then the outro.\n\n### 4.10 Stage format, the Director and the validators\n```js\ndef('stage', 1, { name: 'HARBOR LIGHTS', place: 'Port Lumen Docks', clock: '23:40', music: 'harbor',\n  env: { sky: 'festival', fog: ['#1a2242', 400, 2000], ambient: .38,\n         sun: { dir: [-.35, .45, .82], color: '#a8b8ff', k: .45 }, water: { z: -24, mat: 'sea-night' },\n         weather: null, fireworks: true },\n  build(G) { /* geometry: brushes and prefabs (§5.10) */ },\n  intro: 'c1-intro', outro: 'c1-outro', scenes: ['1-1', '1-2', '1-3', '1-4', '1-B'] });\n\ndef('scene', '1-1', { title: 'LANTERN PIER', time: 45,\n  arrive: { path: [[-160, 0, 24], [-60, -10, 24], [0, -20, 24]], speed: 110,\n            look: [[0, 'path'], [.8, [300, 0, 18]]] },\n  slots: [   // P1 on the left, P2 on the right (facing east, +y is to the right)\n    { stand: { at: [0, -20, 24], look: [300, -10, 16] }, cover: { at: [-6, -32, 12], look: [300, -60, 10] } },\n    { stand: { at: [0, 22, 24], look: [300, 10, 16] },   cover: { at: [-6, 34, 12], look: [300, 60, 10] } } ],\n  covers: { c1: { at: [160, -30, 0], h: 16, peek: ['up'] },\n            c2: { at: [180, 30, 0], h: 16, peek: ['up', 'right'] }, … },\n  waves: [\n    { at: .5, spawn: [['deckhand', 'c1', { entry: 'stand' }], ['deckhand', 'c2'], ['deckhand', 'c3']] },\n    { after: 'clear', spawn: [['deckhand', 'c5', { entry: 'door' }], ['deckhand', 'c4', { entry: 'jump' }]] },\n    { after: 'clear', spawn: [['quartermaster', null, { route: [[360, -80, 0], [360, 90, 0]], drop: 'mg', flee: 3.5 }],\n                              ['deckhand', 'c6', { entry: 'run', from: [420, -60, 0] }]] } ],\n  civilians: [{ at: 2, kind: 'festival-pair', route: [[120, -50, 0], [300, -50, 0]] }],\n  objects: [['lifering', [100, 58, 18]], ['lanterns', 'stalls'], ['bottles', 'c1']],\n  cues: [{ at: 0, say: ['juno', 'Hostiles on the pier! Watch the festival crowd!'] },\n         { on: 'spawn:quartermaster', say: ['juno', \"Orange vest! He's hauling ammo—drop him!\"] }] });\n```\n- **Director phases**: `intro` (cutscene) → `travel` (WAIT) → `action` → `clear` (the time bonus counts up, 1.2 s)\n  → `travel` … → `boss` → `outro` → results.\n- A wave starts by `at` (seconds into the action), `atD` (carrier distance), `after: 'clear' | '<waveId>' | 'kills:N'`,\n  or `on: '<event>'`.\n- A scene clears when every non-civilian it spawned is dead and no wave is pending.\n- Cues: `say` (radio, not modal), `cut` (a short modal line), `prompt` (a tutorial hint, the first time only), `fx`\n  (an explosion, a door, a vehicle), `music` (a layer or a sting).\n- **AI Hub slots 3–4.** Scenes author two slots. Slot 3 = slot 1 moved 28 u further left, perpendicular to the view;\n  slot 4 = slot 2 moved 28 u further right. Each gets an auto cover prop (a crate pair 20 u ahead of its cover pose)\n  unless the scene authors one. The validators check them like real slots.\n\n**Validators** (tests run them over every stage):\n- every id resolves (types, covers, speakers, songs, cutscenes);\n- every cover point's **exposed** head and chest are inside the view (|ax| < 0.95A, |ay| < 0.95) from every slot's\n  stand pose, with a clear ray (no collider in the way). This means every enemy can be shot;\n- every **hidden** pose is fully occluded from every slot (the cover really works);\n- no camera point, sampled along every arrive path and every slot pose, is inside a solid or within 4 u of a wall in\n  front of it;\n- paths are continuous; carriers' slots stay on their carrier;\n- each scene's time allowance is at least 1.3 × the fair bot's clear time (measured by `--playthrough`; the result is\n  stored in `tests/balance.json`);\n- the scene lists in the attract playlist, the dev jump list and the stage lists match.\n\n### 4.11 Story, dialogue and cutscenes\n- **Speakers**: `def('speaker', id, { name, color, rig: { …Humanoid options }, voice: '<voice id>' })`. The portraits\n  are live: `rig.drawPortrait` inside `E.Dialog`.\n- **Cutscene pages** are modal: `talk.say(lines, { name, portrait: rig, onDone })` with the engine's markup:\n  `{c=#ffd36a}…{/c}` for color, `{w=.4}` for a pause, `{shake}…{/shake}` for shouting.\n- **Radio** is not modal: `{ auto: 2.5–4, modal: false, place: 'top' }`, one line at a time and queued. A Subtitles\n  setting hides the text but keeps the voices.\n- **Voice sync.** Set `talk.voice = null`. When a page starts, `Voice.say(text, speakerVoice)` plays it, and the\n  Dialog's typing speed matches the voice's syllable rate.\n- **Cutscene scripts** are generators on the Sim's runner. Steps: `cam(keys)`, `actor(id, { at, path, pose, expr })`,\n  `say(id, text)`, `radio(id, text)`, `wait(s)`, `fx(…)`, `music(…)`.\n  - Press confirm to advance a line; **hold confirm for 0.6 s to skip** (\"HOLD ✕ TO SKIP\" in the corner).\n  - Skipping jumps to the cutscene's end state.\n  - In AI Hub sessions, cutscenes are skipped; the radio and the boss cards stay.\n- **Briefings** (one per stage) are pages over a stylized map of the Lumen Isles drawn with `px`, with Director Hale's\n  and Juno's portraits and two or three objectives. They are skippable.\n\n### 4.12 Audio: music, SFX and voices (everything written in code)\n- **Music** uses the engine tracker `{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes, name, on }] }` (§5.12).\n  - Tracks are named so `audio.layer` can bring the drums and the lead in during ACTION and drop them during WAIT.\n  - `audio.tempo(+8 bpm)` while TIME < 10 s.\n  - Stings (stage clear, boss intro, extra life) via `audio.sting`.\n  - Crossfade between scenes with `music(song, { fade: 1 })`.","numLines":420,"startLine":420,"totalLines":1671}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AwNhMhFcwe11uPN8VQq1WM","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2SB3YvRVoqCiJiTMgW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ECidTbyz27hY1tFmadn94D","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183213__xf5fb84/work/PLAN.md","offset":840,"limit":440},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13109,"cache_read_input_tokens":36858,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":13109},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14efaf47-c831-41be-b2ba-0398106a097f","uuid":"5cf77cae-f313-4cc8-b470-f7d092d38c60","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:32:30.052Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2SApuZzZbJLLgL3mXo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01ECidTbyz27hY1tFmadn94D":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183213__xf5fb84/work/PLAN.md","offset":840,"limit":440}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ECidTbyz27hY1tFmadn94D","type":"tool_result","content":"840\t- **SFX** are registered with `audio.define`. Each weapon is a list of voices: a noise burst with a falling filter plus\n841\t  a sine thump; the explosion is 1.2 s of decaying noise plus a 50-Hz sub. Long or filtered sounds (rain, surf, the\n842\t  crowd, the turbine hum, sluices) are small Web Audio graphs on `audio.bus('sfx')`.\n843\t- **Spatial mix**: pan by screen x, volume `1/(1 + d/240)`. At most 24 voices at once; quieter sounds are dropped first.\n844\t- **Ambience per stage**:\n845\t  - festival: crowd murmur (filtered noise), gibberish cheers, fireworks pops, gulls;\n846\t  - storm: rain, gusts, thunder after each lightning flash, surf on the rocks;\n847\t  - Tidewall: sluice roar, turbine hum, PA announcements in gibberish.\n848\t- **The voice synth** (Animalese/Simlish style, `41-voice.js`):\n849\t  - the text splits into syllables (consonant clusters plus a vowel); each syllable lasts 50–90 ms;\n850\t  - **vowels** drive a sawtooth or pulse source through two parallel band-pass filters at the formants:\n851\t    a 800/1200, e 400/2000, i 300/2300, o 450/800, u 325/700, y 300/1900 Hz;\n852\t  - **consonants** add a short filtered noise burst by class: plosives (p t k b d g) 12 ms clicks; fricatives\n853\t    (s f h z x c) 40 ms hiss; nasals (m n) a 30 ms hum; liquids (l r w) a glide into the vowel;\n854\t  - pitch is the speaker's base × the sentence contour (falling at a \".\", rising at a \"?\", +25% and louder for\n855\t    ALL CAPS or \"!\") × a jitter per letter (a hash ±6%);\n856\t  - pauses: 120 ms at commas, 250 ms at sentence ends;\n857\t  - a per-speaker effect: `radio` (a 300–3000 Hz band and crackle), `boom` (low and wide, with a short delay),\n858\t    `gravel` (40 Hz amplitude modulation, slight drive);\n859\t  - the same text always sounds the same (seeded by its hash).\n860\t  - Barks (\"Hraa!\", \"Reloading!\", \"Ugh!\") use pools of short random syllable strings with no subtitles.\n861\t  - At most 2 dialog voices and 4 barks at once.\n862\t  - Headless (no `audio.ctx`), it schedules nothing and returns the line's duration, which the tests read.\n863\t\n864\t### 4.13 HUD (canvas pixel UI; anchor to `r.W`/`r.H`; keep clear of `game.cornerArea()`)\n865\t- **Top center: TIME.**\n866\t  - \"TIME\" over **`45.00`** (font scale 3), on a 90×28 plate whose water level rises as TIME runs out;\n867\t  - red below 10 s, blinking below 5 s, a tick sound each second;\n868\t  - the boss bar goes under it.\n869\t- **Top left: SCORE** (scale 2), with the chain \"×1.4\" under it.\n870\t- **Bottom left: LIFE**, a row of CREST shield-badge sprites that flash and break when lost.\n871\t- **Bottom right: the weapon panel.**\n872\t  - icon, name and ammo: 9 bullet pips for the handgun; a number and a bar for specials;\n873\t  - a reload ring while in cover;\n874\t  - a 4-slot strip shows for 0.8 s after a switch.\n875\t- **Center**:\n876\t  - the reticle in the player's color with a white outline (styles: cross, ring, dot; sizes S/M/L);\n877\t  - a hit marker (a white ×, gold for a headshot);\n878\t  - \"RELOAD!\" under the ammo and flashing over the reticle when empty;\n879\t  - **\"ACTION!\"** (scale 4, slides in, 0.9 s) and **\"WAIT\"** (scale 3, blinking);\n880\t  - \"TIME EXTEND +2\", \"TIDE'S IN!\", \"STAGE CLEAR\";\n881\t  - score popups (\"+1500\") at the projected enemy positions;\n882\t  - pickup banners (\"MACHINE GUN +60\");\n883\t  - \"NEW WEAPON\" cards the first time a weapon is picked up.\n884\t- **Threat rings and edge arrows**, as in §4.7.\n885\t- **Cover feedback**: a dark vignette, a small \"COVER\" tag at the bottom center, the reticle hidden.\n886\t- **Radio box**: at the top, with a 32-px live portrait, the name in the speaker's color, two lines of at most 40\n887\t  characters, auto-advancing.\n888\t- **Prompt bar** (bottom center): device-aware tutorial prompts the first time (\"HOLD [L2] TO TAKE COVER\").\n889\t- **Co-op**:\n890\t  - split screen: each viewport has its own HUD. Secondary text uses scale 1, but key numbers never go below scale 2.\n891\t  - AI Hub: a teammate strip at the top right (name in the seat color, lives, a ● when in cover).\n892\t- **10-foot sizes**: the smallest text is scale 1 (7 px of 240, about 31 px on a 1080p TV); key numbers are scale 2–3.\n893\t  Check 16:9, 4:3 and 21:9.\n894\t\n895\t### 4.14 Screens, flow and the 10-foot UI\n896\t**Rules**:\n897\t- at most 7 items a screen, each with a one-line description under it;\n898\t- one big focus highlight, animated as a wave;\n899\t- d-pad, stick or arrows move the focus, and hovering the mouse moves it too; ✕, Enter or a click picks; ○, Esc or a\n900\t  right click goes back;\n901\t- no dense tables: the results show 6 big count-up stats, and lists are paged (L1/R1, the wheel, or arrows), never\n902\t  scrolled.\n903\t\n904\tUse `E.Menu` (it already does hover, click and keys), drawn with our own frame (`E.ui.box`, gradient\n905\t`['#10244a', '#081428']`, accent `#3aa0e8`, text `#f4f7ff`, gold `#ffd36a`, warning `#ff3b30`).\n906\t\n907\t```\n908\tboot → title ─(any button)→ menu ─┬ ARCADE → difficulty → briefing(1) → play(1) → results → briefing(2) … → ending → initials → records → title\n909\t                                  ├ CO-OP → coop join (P2 press START on a pad) → difficulty → (as ARCADE, split screen)\n910\t                                  ├ STAGE SELECT → 3 cards → difficulty → briefing(n) → play(n) → results → initials → title\n911\t                                  ├ TRAINING → play(range) → range results → menu\n912\t                                  ├ RECORDS (tables per mode · range best · INTEL)   ├ SETTINGS (engine screens)\n913\t                                  └ ABOUT (pages)                                     └ HOW TO PLAY (device-aware diagram)\n914\tplay: pause (engine menu: Resume · Restart scene · Restart stage · How to play · Dev mode · Settings · Controls · Full screen · Quit to title)\n915\ttitle/menu idle 10 s → attract (§4.17)          AI Hub: boot → session card → play(round stage) → session results\n916\t```\n917\t**The screens**:\n918\t- **Title**: a slow flyover of Stage 1's night harbor with fireworks, rendered by Rail3D.\n919\t  - The logo is `E.font.title` \"TIDE CRISIS\" (scale 4; colors `#ffffff #9fe3ff #3aa0e8 #1a4f9c`; outline `#08142a`;\n920\t    depth 3) with a big \"3\" in `#fff3b0 #ffb347 #ff5a2a #a81e1e` and an animated wave band under it.\n921\t  - \"PRESS ✕\" for a pad, \"PRESS ENTER OR CLICK\" for keyboard and mouse.\n922\t  - `E.versionLabel()` small, at the bottom.\n923\t- **Touch-only** devices (coarse pointer, no fine pointer, no gamepad, no key pressed yet): the title shows **\"Controller\n924\t  or keyboard required\"** and a large gear button that opens `game.menu('settings')`. There are no touch controls\n925\t  (`touch: []`, `input.touchEnabled = false`). The attract mode runs. A key press or a connected pad unlocks the\n926\t  normal title.\n927\t- **Briefing**: map pages; skip with ✕ or Enter.\n928\t- **Results**: the stage name, then count-ups (time, accuracy, kills, headshots, cover saves, lives lost), the time\n929\t  bonus, the accuracy bonus (`accuracy% × 300`), the no-damage bonus (20,000) and the total. The rank letter lands\n930\t  with a shake.\n931\t- **Game over**: \"GAME OVER\" → initials on `E.Arcade`'s letter wheel if the score qualifies → the table.\n932\t- **Ending**: the cutscene, the credits roll (cast with live portraits, \"Made with my-3D2dge\", the music), the final\n933\t  score, the initials.\n934\t- **Records**: tables `arcade`, `coop`, `stage1`–`stage3` (via `E.Arcade.table/qualifies/enter/drawTable`), the range's\n935\t  best time, and INTEL (the bottles found, each with its short funny note).\n936\t- **About**, as pages:\n937\t  1. the game and its credits;\n938\t  2. **Source Information**;\n939\t  3. **Testing Feedback**;\n940\t  4. **Engine Feedback**;\n941\t  5. **Prompt Feedback**;\n942\t  6. thanks and licenses (the engine is MIT; the asset store is CC0 if used).\n943\t\n944\t  All of it comes from `src/99-about.js`.\n945\t\n946\t### 4.15 Settings (our items, added through `menu.settings` beside the engine's)\n947\t| Key | Label | Options (default) |\n948\t|---|---|---|\n949\t| `difficulty` | Difficulty (new runs) | Easy / **Normal** / Hard |\n950\t| `aimAssist` | Aim assist (controller) | Off / Low / **Medium** / High |\n951\t| `padSpeed` | Aim speed (controller) | slider 0.5–2.0 (**1.0**) |\n952\t| `coverMode` | Cover control | **Hold** / Toggle |\n953\t| `autoFire` | Hold fire to repeat (handgun) | **On** / Off |\n954\t| `reticle` / `reticleSize` | Reticle / size | **Cross** / Ring / Dot · S / **M** / L |\n955\t| `threatSize` | Crisis rings | **Normal** / Large |\n956\t| `subtitles` | Subtitles | **On** / Off |\n957\t| `wobble` | Retro texture wobble | **Off** / On |\n958\t\n959\tThe engine already provides Button symbols (patched to PlayStation by default, with Xbox), volumes, comfort settings\n960\t(shake and flashes, which our FX respect), screen filters (CRT, scanlines), dead zone, invert Y, vibration and\n961\trebinding. Its Controls screen names our actions: `menu.controls = { fire: 'Fire', cover: 'Take cover', next: 'Next weapon', prev: 'Previous weapon', reload: 'Quick reload', w1: 'Handgun', … }`.\n962\t\n963\t### 4.16 Dev mode (from the pause menu; not available in AI Hub sessions)\n964\t\"Dev mode\" in the pause menu resumes the engine and opens our **canvas dev panel**. The Sim is frozen while the panel\n965\tis open; close it to play with the new values. It has 6 tabs (L1/R1, Q/E or a click):\n966\t\n967\t1. **PLAYER**: God mode · Infinite ammo · Start lives (1–9, 5) · I-frames (0.5–3 s, 1.5) · Cover in (0.05–0.4, 0.12) ·\n968\t   Cover out (0.05–0.4, 0.15) · Reload (0.1–1.0, 0.30) · Pad aim speed (0.5–3, 1.6) · Aim assist (0–1, .6).\n969\t2. **WEAPONS**: handgun damage (0.5–3, 1) · MG rate (4–20/s, 12) · MG damage (0.1–1, .45) · shotgun pellets (4–14, 8) ·\n970\t   shotgun spread (2–12°, 5.5) · GL radius (24–96, 48) · GL damage (2–12, 6) · Ammo pickups × (0.5–3, 1).\n971\t3. **ENEMIES**: HP × (0.5–3, 1) · Threat chance × (0–3, 1) · Telegraph × (0.5–2, 1) · Fire interval × (0.5–2, 1) ·\n972\t   Max threats (1–4) · Minimum gap (0.1–1 s, .35) · Reaction delay × (0.5–2, 1) · Boss HP × (0.25–3, 1).\n973\t4. **TIME**: Freeze timer · Scene time × (0.5–2, 1) · Elite bonus (0–5 s, 2) · Refill after time-up (20–100%, 60) ·\n974\t   Game speed (0.25–1.5, 1).\n975\t5. **VIEW**: FOV (45–75, 55) · Show hit volumes · Colliders · Rail and cover points · AI state labels · Threat debug ·\n976\t   Perf overlay (ms per pass, triangles, pixels, actors) · Free camera · Hide HUD.\n977\t6. **ACTIONS**: Restart scene · Next / previous scene · Jump to… (every scene, boss and cutscene) · Kill all · Spawn\n978\t   enemy (pick a type; it appears at the nearest free cover point) · All weapons and full ammo · +1 life · Lose a life ·\n979\t   Trigger a threat on me · Clear stage · Game over · Bot plays (Autopilot) · Add a P2 bot (split-screen test) ·\n980\t   Voice test (each speaker says a line) · Music test (every song and layer).\n981\t\n982\t**RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits at the bottom, with a confirmation. □ or Backspace resets the focused item.\n983\t- Changed values show in gold, with the default in grey.\n984\t- Navigation: up/down move; left/right adjust (holding accelerates); ✕ or Enter toggles or runs; ○ or Esc closes.\n985\t  Hovering the mouse focuses, dragging moves a slider's knob, and the wheel adjusts.\n986\t- Values live in `tune` for the visit, and Reset copies `TUNE` back.\n987\t- Every slider's key is a path into `tune` (`'enemy.hpMul'`), and the panel is generated from a descriptor list, so\n988\t  adding a variable takes one line.\n989\t\n990\t### 4.17 Attract mode and the Autopilot\n991\t**The attract controller** (`53-attract.js`):\n992\t- **Trigger**: 10 s on the title or the main menu with no input. Input means any key or button, a pointer move of more\n993\t  than 4 px, the wheel, or a stick past its dead zone.\n994\t- **The cycle**: DEMO (highlight *i*, about 28 s) → HIGH SCORES (6 s) → DEMO (*i+1*) → TITLE (8 s) → repeat, carrying\n995\t  on through the playlist.\n996\t- **The playlist** (`def('highlight', …)`), in order:\n997\t  1. 1-1 Lantern Pier\n998\t  2. 1-4 Canal Run\n999\t  3. 1-B Barracuda\n1000\t  4. 2-1 Gull's Rest\n1001\t  5. 2-3 Funicular and The Gull\n1002\t  6. 2-B Hammerhead\n1003\t  7. 3-2 Sluice Locks and Seawolf\n1004\t  8. 3-3 Turbine Hall\n1005\t  9. 3-B Scylla and Krake\n1006\t- **The demo** is `Sim({ mode: 'attract' })` jumped to the highlight. It cheats freely: god mode, infinite ammo, travel\n1007\t  ×1.5. Telegraphs still show, so the mechanic is visible. The bot plays in `showoff` style.\n1008\t- The overlay shows \"DEMO PLAY\", the small logo and a blinking \"PRESS START\" in the right device label. The HUD stays.\n1009\t- **Any input** goes back to the title, and that input is consumed so it doesn't also pick a menu item.\n1010\t- **Watchdog**: if nothing progresses for 8 s, move to the next highlight.\n1011\t- On touch-only devices the attract mode runs the same way; a tap goes back to the title, which shows the notice.\n1012\t\n1013\t**The Autopilot** produces the same `Command` objects a player does. It drives tests (`fair`), the attract mode\n1014\t(`showoff`) and the dev \"P2 bot\".\n1015\t- **Perception**: it reads the Sim state, including threats and their timers.\n1016\t- **Target priority**:\n1017\t  1. threats on me, soonest impact first;\n1018\t  2. projectiles in flight;\n1019\t  3. Red Tide and snipers;\n1020\t  4. rushers, nearest first;\n1021\t  5. shields, if a shotgun or GL is available;\n1022\t  6. the rest, by exposure time left.\n1023\t\n1024\t  Never civilians, hostages or allies. When idle: life rings, bottles, and barrels next to enemies.\n1025\t- **Aim**: a critically damped spring toward the target's head (headshot chance by skill) or chest, with a maximum\n1026\t  speed and a reaction delay. It fires when the projected reticle is inside the target's volume, with jitter by skill.\n1027\t- **Cover**: hide when a threat on me has under 0.25 s left and its source won't die first; hide to reload when empty\n1028\t  (or at ≤ 2 rounds with no threats); pop out when no impact is due within 0.3 s.\n1029\t- **Weapons**: MG against drones and groups; the shotgun against rushers, shields and close range; the GL against\n1030\t  vehicles, barricades and bosses' armored parts; otherwise the handgun.\n1031\t- **Skills**:\n1032\t\n1033\t| Skill | Reaction | Speed | Headshots |\n1034\t|---|---|---|---|\n1035\t| `fair` | 0.30 s | 2.2 vu/s | 30% |\n1036\t| `expert` | 0.18 s | 3.5 vu/s | 60% |\n1037\t| `showoff` | `expert`, and uses every weapon and bonus target | | |\n1038\t\n1039\t### 4.18 Local co-op (split screen)\n1040\t- **CO-OP** opens a join screen: \"PLAYER 2 — PRESS START ON A CONTROLLER\".\n1041\t  - `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`; the new player is `game.addPlayer({ pad: i })`.\n1042\t  - P1 keeps the keyboard and mouse and any other pad. The mouse always belongs to P1.\n1043\t  - In ARCADE, a second pad's Start also drops P2 in during play, with a slide into split screen.\n1044\t- `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'columns' })`. Rail3D renders each viewport with that player's camera, and the HUD is\n1045\t  drawn per viewport.\n1046\t- One Sim with two players: shared enemies, time and rail; separate slots, cover, aim, weapons, ammo, lives, score\n1047\t  and continues.\n1048\t  - Threats choose a target player.\n1049\t  - Pickups go to the player who made the kill.\n1050\t  - Life rings go to whoever shoots them.\n1051\t- When a player is out, their half shows \"CONTINUE? 10\" while the other plays on. If both are out, it's game over.\n1052\t\n1053\t### 4.19 AI Hub online co-op (shared world; follow `lib/hub/README.md`, protocol 1)\n1054\t**Tags** in `<head>`, after the session client:\n1055\t```html\n1056\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1057\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n1058\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n1059\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Clear the stage together; most points wins\">\n1060\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim mouse/stick · Fire click/R2 · Hold right-click/L2: cover+reload\">\n1061\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"7\">\n1062\t```\n1063\tThe goal must stay at 60 characters or fewer and the howto at 80 or fewer.\n1064\t\n1065\t**Flow** when `AIHub.active`:\n1066\t1. Skip the title, the attract mode and every menu. Use `new E.Game({ autoPause: false, corner: false, fullscreen: false, … })`,\n1067\t   and make the play scene not pausable.\n1068\t2. In the first boot step, call\n1069\t   `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`,\n1070\t   carry on as a normal game.\n1071\t3. Build the round's stage: **stage = ((info.round − 1) mod 3) + 1**. Seats map to agents in seat order: Kai, Rook,\n1072\t   Mari, Juno, and the colors come from `info.players`.\n1073\t4. Call `AIHub.ready()` with no key press.\n1074\t5. Show **our own card**, the session briefing: the stage name and art, every player in their color with a READY tick,\n1075\t   the goal, this device's controls, and the countdown from the `card` event.\n1076\t   - Enter or ✕ (button 0) calls `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n1077\t   - △ (button 3) or M calls `AIHub.answer('more')`; answering again takes it back.\n1078\t   - The card has no menus or choices, and draws no `Math.random`.\n1079\t6. On `start`, count down 3-2-1 while `AIHub.time() < 0`; ACTION! at 0. No intro cutscene.\n1080\t7. **Host** (`info.isHost`) runs the authoritative Sim with every player.\n1081\t   - **Server** (`AIHub.server`): no local player, input or camera. Show a status screen (\"HOSTING · 3 AGENTS ·\n1082\t     AREA 1-3\") and keep the world running.\n1083\t8. The end:\n1084\t   - the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })` once: on the boss's defeat, when every\n1085\t     player has left, or immediately on `time-up`;\n1086\t   - `standings` group equal scores; `won` is true if the boss was defeated;\n1087\t   - stats per player: Kills, Accuracy, Headshots, Cover saves, Lives lost;\n1088\t   - awards: Sharpshooter (best accuracy), Pointman (most kills), Iron Wall (most cover saves);\n1089\t   - on `end`: stop input (`input.clear()`, ignore everything after), `audio.stopMusic()` and mute, and show \"ROUND OVER\"\n1090\t     (or \"HOST LEFT — ROUND VOID\", or \"YOU SAT THIS ONE OUT\").\n1091\t9. **Rules**:\n1092\t   - network only through `AIHub.send`;\n1093\t   - no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation;\n1094\t   - Quit, Restart and Title do nothing;\n1095\t   - Options while Create is held is ignored;\n1096\t   - names are only ever drawn to the canvas, never into HTML.\n1097\t\n1098\t**Messages** (short keys, quantized integers):\n1099\t\n1100\t| Direction | Message | Reliability, rate | Content |\n1101\t|---|---|---|---|\n1102\t| client → host | `in` | unreliable, 15/s | `{ t: 'in', at, a: [ax·1000, ay·1000], c: 0/1, w }` |\n1103\t| client → host | `sh` | reliable, ≤ 8/s, only when something happened | shots `[[at, w, ax, ay], …]` and cover changes `[[at, c], …]` since the last |\n1104\t| host → all | `s` | unreliable, 10/s, **≤ 1,100 bytes** | `{ t: 's', at, ph, sc, d, tm, p: [[id, score, lives, chain, w, ammo, c]], e: [[id, type, x, y, z, yaw, state, hp]], pr: [[id, kind, x, y, z]], b: [phase, …hp] }` |\n1105\t| host → all | `ev` | reliable, ≤ 8/s, batched | spawn, kill (with the shooter), hit, threat (`{ id, src, target, t0, dur }` in hub time), pickup, cue, scene, clear, over |\n1106\t\n1107\t- **Rate limit**: a token bucket of 18 messages per second per copy (the protocol caps it at 20). Skip a snapshot when\n1108\t  the bucket is empty; reliable batches merge.\n1109\t- **The host**:\n1110\t  - applies each player's latest `in` and every `sh` shot, rewinding the enemies: it keeps a 300-ms ring buffer of hit\n1111\t    volumes at 30 Hz and tests each shot against the sample nearest its `at`, clamped 0–250 ms into the past;\n1112\t  - keeps a cover timeline per player;\n1113\t  - resolves each threat at T + 0.2 s against the cover state that player reported at T.\n1114\t- **The replica** (a client):\n1115\t  - renders enemies interpolated at `AIHub.time() − 120 ms`, driving rigs from position, velocity and state codes;\n1116\t  - draws threat rings from `threat` events on the hub's timeline;\n1117\t  - predicts its own cover, weapon and ammo at once and corrects them when the snapshot disagrees for 0.5 s;\n1118\t  - takes score and lives from the host;\n1119\t  - shows its own shots' sparks and hit markers at once, against the interpolated volumes, as cosmetics only; the\n1120\t    host's `kill` events decide.\n1121\t- **When a player leaves**: their agent disappears and threats retarget. If the host leaves, the clients get\n1122\t  `end: host-left`.\n1123\t- A snapshot over budget drops the enemies furthest from every camera; they come round-robin in the next snapshot.\n1124\t\n1125\t### 4.20 Records, saves and intel\n1126\t- High-score tables go through `E.Arcade` (modes `arcade`, `coop`, `stage1`–`stage3`). Don't use `E.Arcade.add`: it\n1127\t  floats points in 2D world space. Use only `table`, `qualifies`, `enter` and `drawTable`.\n1128\t- The range's best time, the flags (`seenTraining`, `seenPrompts.*`) and the INTEL bottles found (6: two per stage) go\n1129\t  in `E.store`.\n1130\t- There are no mid-run saves; this is an arcade game. STAGE SELECT makes every stage reachable.\n1131\t- In AI Hub sessions, storage is the client's memory-only storage, so nothing persists. That's fine.\n1132\t\n1133\t---\n1134\t\n1135\t## 5. Content\n1136\t\n1137\t### 5.1 World and cast\n1138\tThe **Lumen Isles** are an island republic whose capital, **Port Lumen**, lies below sea level behind the **Meridian\n1139\tTidewall**, a 3-km tidal barrier and power plant. Tonight is the **Lantern Festival**, on the eve of the **king tide**,\n1140\tthe highest in eighteen years, which peaks at **06:00**. **Admiral Silas Krake**, court-martialed after a storm\n1141\tdisaster he blames on the city, leads the **Black Current** mercenary fleet. He has seized the harbor and the\n1142\tTidewall, and at the peak he will open all forty floodgates.\n1143\t\n1144\t| Character | Role | Look (Humanoid options) | Voice |\n1145\t|---|---|---|---|\n1146\t| **Kai Navarro** (P1, seat 1, red) | CREST agent; calm, fond of one-liners | `heroic`, size 1.05; charcoal suit coat, red tie (trim), short black hair; `gun` | 150 Hz, saw, 11 syllables/s |\n1147\t| **Rook Halvorsen** (P2, seat 2, blue) | CREST demolitions, ex-navy diver; gruff; calls Kai \"kid\" | `bulky`, 1.1; brown leather coat, blue shirt, bald | 105 Hz, `gravel`, 9/s |\n1148\t| **Mari Tejada** (P3, seat 3, green) | Lieutenant, Lumen Coast Guard; drives the boats | `heroic`, 1.0; orange and white coast-guard jacket with green trim, ponytail | 230 Hz, bright, 12/s |\n1149\t| **Juno Achterberg** (P4, seat 4, yellow) | CREST tech on the radio (in hub sessions, a field agent) | `chibi`, 1.0; yellow headset (band hat), spiky teal hair | 200 Hz, nasal, `radio`, 13/s |\n1150\t| **Director Ines Hale** | CREST HQ; briefings | `heroic`, 1.0; grey coat, long silver hair | 175 Hz, `radio`, 10/s |\n1151\t| **Dr. Tomas Tejada** | Tidewall chief engineer; a hostage | white lab coat, short grey hair | 140 Hz, warm, 9/s |\n1152\t| **Admiral Silas Krake** | Villain; the final boss | `heroic`, 1.15; long navy greatcoat with gold trim, cape, long white hair, naval cap; `cutlass` + `flare` | 95 Hz, `boom`, 8/s |\n1153\t| **Brigitte \"Barracuda\" Sorel** | Stage 1 boss, captain of the gunboat *Stingray* | teal wetsuit with armor, white ponytail; `harpoon`, then `cutlass` | 210 Hz, staccato, 13/s |\n1154\t| **Bruno \"Hammerhead\" Kessler** | Stage 2 boss, the enforcer | `bulky`, 1.6; armor, flat-topped steel helmet; `minigun` + `hatch` shield | 80 Hz, `gravel`, 6/s, often in CAPS |\n1155\t| **The Gull** | Stage 2 sniper mini-boss | a hooded grey coat; `sniper` with a laser | 190 Hz, whispery |\n1156\t\n1157\tThe vehicles and machines are the **Stingray** gunboat, the **Bulldog** armored truck, the **Seawolf** gunship\n1158\thelicopter and **Scylla**, a six-legged crab walker.\n1159\t\n1160\t### 5.2 Tone guide (the source's B-movie style; original lines)\n1161\t- Short, punchy, earnest. Names get said a lot, and exclamation marks are common. At most 70 characters a page, and at\n1162\t  most two lines.\n1163\t- Heroes trade cool one-liners and their partner groans at them. HQ talks in urgent radio jargon (\"Copy that\",\n1164\t  \"HQ to Kai\").\n1165\t- The villain is grandiose and speaks in sea metaphors. Henchmen announce themselves.\n1166\t- Keep it slightly over the top, as 2000s arcade localization was, but never broken English, and never mocking.\n1167\t\n1168\t### 5.3 Enemy roster\n1169\t| id | Look | HP | Entries | Peek | Exposed for | Fire | Threat chance / telegraph | Score | Notes |\n1170\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1171\t| `deckhand` | navy sweater, watch cap, `rifle`; 4 skin tones | 1 | stand, run, jump, door | up, side | 1.4–2.2 s | burst of 3 at 0.12 s | 25% (S1) → 40% (S3) / 1.10 s | 1,000 | the common grunt |\n1172\t| `marine` | olive armor, helmet, `rifle` | 2 | run, rappel, vault | up, side | 1.6–2.4 | burst of 4 | 35% / 0.95 | 1,500 | flinches on the first hit |\n1173\t| `redtide` | crimson coat, beret, balaclava, `smg` | 2 | roll, jump | side | 0.9–1.3 | burst of 2 | **100% / 0.55** | 3,000, +2 s | radio warns the first time |\n1174\t| `quartermaster` | **orange life vest**, crate on the back (`offhand: 'crate'`) | 1 | runs a route across | — | 2.5–4 s crossing | rarely: 10% / 1.2 | — | 2,000 | drops the scene's ammo; flees |\n1175\t| `grenadier` | `bulky`, bandolier | 2 | stand, run | up | 1.2 | lobs a grenade | the grenade lands as a threat | 2,000 | the grenade can be shot down |\n1176\t| `rocketeer` | helmet, goggles, `launcher` | 2 | stand (high) | up, side | 2.0 aim | a rocket | 100% (aim glint 0.8 + flight) | 2,500 | the rocket can be shot down |\n1177\t| `shield` | `bulky`, riot shield, `smg` | 3 | advances between covers | over | — | burst of 3 | 40% / 0.9 | 3,000 | immune from the front; a shotgun or GL hit drops the shield aside for 1.5 s; head open when he fires |\n1178\t| `rusher` | tank top, bandana, `cutlass` | 1.5 | sprints in a zigzag | — | 2–3 s approach | slashes at 2 m | 100% / 0.45 wind-up | 2,000 | cover blocks the slash; he staggers back and runs in again |\n1179\t| `sniper` | hooded, `sniper` | 1 | stand (far, high) | up | long | 1 shot | 100% / 1.4 (the laser converges) | 3,000, +2 s | the beam is visible in the world |\n1180\t| `drone` | a small quadrotor mesh with a red eye light | 2 | flies a path | — | erratic | zap beam | 30% / 0.9 | 1,500 | falls in sparks |\n1181\t| `jetski` | a rider on a jet ski | rider 1 / ski 3 | races a water path | — | a pass | pistol | 30% / 1.0 | 1,500 (+1,000 for the ski) | the ski's explosion kills the rider |\n1182\t| `gunner` | a mounted gun (bridge or truck) | 3 | stand | — | — | long bursts | 50% / 1.0 | 2,500 | |\n1183\t| `captor` | Red Tide holding a hostage | 1 (head only) | stand | side (head) | 0.7 s | pistol | 100% / 0.8 | 5,000 | hitting the hostage = −1 life |\n1184\t| `civilian` | festival-goers, dock workers, fishermen | 1 | run, hide | — | — | — | — | −5,000 | panics and crouches |\n1185\t\n1186\t### 5.4 Stage 1, HARBOR LIGHTS (Port Lumen Docks, 23:40, Lantern Festival)\n1187\t**Look**:\n1188\t- night blue; the sky goes `#0b1026 → #1b2a52 → #3a3060` with a magenta city glow at the horizon; fog `#1a2242`\n1189\t  400–2000;\n1190\t- warm paper lanterns (`#ffb347`, static lights of radius 90);\n1191\t- festival string lights (emissive `#ff5a5a #ffd34a #5ad1ff #8aff8a`);\n1192\t- fireworks bursting over the bay, each flash a dynamic light;\n1193\t- dark water with lantern sparkles.\n1194\t\n1195\tMusic: \"Harbor Lights\".\n1196\t\n1197\t| Scene | Time | Setting and cover | Waves (about 8–14 enemies) | New in this scene | Objects and cues |\n1198\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1199\t| **1-0 intro** | — | Mari's speedboat races in under fireworks; Krake's broadcast plays on the festival's giant screens (a live portrait texture); they dock at Lantern Pier | — | — | lines in §5.9 |\n1200\t| **1-1 LANTERN PIER** | 45 | A 7.5-m wooden pier lined with lantern stalls; the warehouses of the quay ahead (x 380). P1 hides behind stacked fish crates, P2 behind barrels and a bollard (the schema example in §4.10) | (a) 3 deckhands pop up behind stalls; (b) one comes out of the warehouse door, one jumps off a fishing boat's cabin roof; (c) a quartermaster crosses the quay (MG +60) and a deckhand runs in | cover, reload, the handgun, the MG pickup, civilians | a festival couple flees early (don't shoot); breakable lanterns and bottles; **life ring** on a post; first-time prompts for cover and reload |\n1201\t| **1-2 FISH MARKET** | 50 | Travel: along the quay, then Rook kicks in a side door. Inside, a brick hall with a tiled floor, wooden stalls, ice tables, hanging paper lanterns and an upper gallery | (a) 4 deckhands, 2 of them on the gallery, who fall when killed; (b) **the first Red Tide** rolls out from behind the ice table; (c) 2 marines vault the stalls, and a grenadier on the gallery throws the **first grenade** | Red Tide, grenades, explosive red generator tanks under the gallery (shooting them drops a section of it, killing 2) | an **intel bottle** on a shelf; Rook visible fighting at the far end |\n1202\t| **1-3 CONTAINER YARD** | 60 | Berth 9: container stacks (red, blue, ochre, green), a gantry crane, sodium lamps, forklifts | (a) 3 deckhands jump down off containers and 2 hide behind forklifts; (b) **BULLDOG**, an armored truck, smashes through a container wall: its gunner (3 HP, above the gun shield, 50% threat), then its fuel tank (6 HP) for a huge blast; 2 marines climb out of the back; (c) a grenadier and a quartermaster (MG) | a vehicle set piece | a dock worker hides behind a forklift (\"Don't shoot! I just work here!\") |\n1203\t| **1-4 CANAL RUN** (moving) | 60 | Mari drives through the Silver Canals under festival bridges; duck behind the boat's armored gunwale (part of the carrier); spray, sway | 3 pairs of jet skis from side canals; bridge gunners on 2 bridges, with deckhands on the railings who fall into the water; a quartermaster on a moored boat | a moving fight, jet skis | a fireworks barge: shoot its crates and the rockets wreck an enemy boat; a low bridge (\"Heads down!\", purely visual) |\n1204\t| **1-B BOSS: STINGRAY → BARRACUDA** | 100 | The harbor mouth, the two boats side by side; cover behind the gunwale | §5.7 | the boss framework, torpedoes | — |\n1205\t| **1-Z outro** | — | Barracuda falls into the sea; the Stingray explodes; Juno traces Krake's signal | — | — | STAGE CLEAR → results |\n1206\t\n1207\t### 5.5 Stage 2, STORM SIGNAL (Gull's Rest and Lighthouse Point, 02:10, thunderstorm)\n1208\t**Look**:\n1209\t- slate and green-grey, with heavy rain (screen streaks and splashes);\n1210\t- lightning every 9–15 s: the sky flashes, a white `game.flash` at 0.25, thunder after 0.5–1.5 s;\n1211\t- fog `#26343c` 200–1200;\n1212\t- timber and plaster houses with slate roofs; wet stone (a `wet` overlay pattern that brightens lit bands);\n1213\t- fishermen in yellow raincoats;\n1214\t- the lighthouse beam sweeps every 8 s: a visible cone of glowing lines and a dynamic light.\n1215\t\n1216\tMusic: \"Storm Signal\".\n1217\t\n1218\t| Scene | Time | Setting and cover | Waves | New | Objects and cues |\n1219\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1220\t| **2-0 intro** | — | The boat docks in the rain; lightning shows the lighthouse; Krake's radio intercept and Hammerhead's reply | — | — | — |\n1221\t| **2-1 GULL'S REST** | 50 | A steep cobbled lane, timber houses, washing lines, boats up on blocks; cover behind a fish cart and a stone well | (a) deckhands in windows (the glass breaks); (b) **rushers**, 2 then 3, sprinting down the lane; (c) a quartermaster from an alley drops the **SHOTGUN +8** (\"NEW WEAPON\"); marines behind a cart barricade | rushers, the shotgun | a fisherman ducks in a doorway; laundry that tears and swings |\n1222\t| **2-2 NET LOFTS** | 55 | Inside a tall wooden loft: stairs, hanging nets (`alphaTest`), fish-oil barrels | (a) a **shield** trooper advances down the stairs (\"Shotgun! Knock that shield aside!\"); (b) 3 **rappellers** drop through a roof hatch; (c) 2 Red Tide and a grenadier on the upper floor | shields, rappelling | the fish-oil barrels explode into fire; an **intel bottle** |\n1223\t| **2-3 FUNICULAR** (moving) | 60 | The cliff funicular climbs through the storm; duck behind the car's bench and bulkhead | deckhands on the cliff-path terraces; **the passing car**: the descending car crosses with 3 gunners in a 4-s window; at the top station the car stops for **THE GULL**: a laser sniper among 3 perches on the lighthouse gallery, 4 HP (a headshot does 2) | snipers and laser telegraphs; a mini-boss | a quartermaster at the top station (MG); the **life ring** |\n1224\t| **2-4 LIGHTHOUSE KEEP** | 60 | The courtyard, the keeper's house, the lighthouse door; then up the **spiral stairs** (vertical travel; shoot upward) | (a) marines and a Red Tide; (b) **hostage**: a captor holds Dr. Tejada at the door (\"One more step and the old man swims!\"); his head shows for 0.7-s windows; (c) rushers and deckhands through the house windows; 2 deckhands on the stairs above | hostages | rescuing Dr. Tejada is a cue |\n1225\t| **2-B BOSS: HAMMERHEAD** | 120 | The lantern gallery at the top: the giant rotating lens, the storm, cover in the doorway and then behind the lens housing | §5.7 | cover that degrades and a scripted cover swap | — |\n1226\t| **2-Z outro** | — | Dr. Tejada reveals the override is in the control tower; Juno: take the maintenance tram | — | — | results |\n1227\t\n1228\t### 5.6 Stage 3, THE TIDEWALL (05:20 → 06:00, dawn)\n1229\t**Look**:\n1230\t- dawn: violet, then orange; the sky goes `#1f1a3a → #6a3f6f → #f29e6b` and the sun rises over the stage; fog\n1231\t  `#8a7a8f` 500–2400;\n1232\t- concrete slab, steel, hazard stripes (`#f2c12e`/`#1b1b1b`), sodium lamps (`#ffb04a`);\n1233\t- teal water that **rises** through the stage: the water z goes up 1.5 u per scene and 4 u in the final fight;\n1234\t- white foam at the sluices; the turbine hall's emissive screens (`#43f0c8`).\n1235\t\n1236\tMusic: \"Tidewall\".\n1237\t\n1238\t| Scene | Time | Setting and cover | Waves | New | Objects and cues |\n1239\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1240\t| **3-0 intro** | — | The maintenance tram runs along the crest, the sea high on one side and the city low on the other; Krake on the barrier's giant screens | — | — | — |\n1241\t| **3-1 CREST ROAD** | 50 | The crest road, gate-machinery houses, lamp posts, a barricade | (a) a swarm of 5 **drones** (\"Use the machine gun!\"); (b) 2 **rocketeers** on gantries (\"Rocket! Shoot it down!\"); (c) a quartermaster drops the **GRENADE LAUNCHER +2**; marines and a shield behind a barricade (GL blasts it apart) | drones, rockets, the GL | — |\n1242\t| **3-2 SLUICE LOCKS** | 70 | Catwalks over the roaring sluices; the giant gate | **4 glowing gate locks**, 6 HP each, to jam before the tide gauge peaks (a scene-local gauge); **SEAWOLF**, a gunship helicopter, strafes (threat bursts), drops rappellers and retreats after 30 damage; deckhands and Red Tide on the catwalks; drones | an objective set piece | Dr. Tejada on the radio guides you |\n1243\t| **3-3 TURBINE HALL** | 60 | An interior: spinning turbines (dynamic meshes), steam vents, cranes, catwalks | (a) marines and Red Tide on catwalks; (b) 2 shields and rushers; (c) a grenadier and a rocketeer | combinations of everything | coolant tanks (explosive, white vapor); an **intel bottle**; Rook across the hall |\n1244\t| **3-4 TOWER LIFT** (moving) | 50 | The glass elevator on the outside of the control tower; duck behind the lift's console | drones; rappellers on the lift roof; **SEAWOLF returns**: 2 weapon pods (10 HP each), then the rotor hub (15 HP), and it crashes into the sea | — | the **life ring** on the lift |\n1245\t| **3-B FINAL: SCYLLA → KRAKE** | 90 + 90 | The tower roof and helipad (behind a concrete parapet), then the control room (behind the consoles) | §5.7 | the **FINAL SHOT** | — |\n1246\t| **3-Z ending** | — | The gates close, the sunrise floods in, Krake is arrested, then the credits | — | — | the ending and the credits roll |\n1247\t\n1248\t### 5.7 Bosses (patterns)\n1249\t**STINGRAY → BARRACUDA** (1-B, 100 s):\n1250\t1. **The Stingray, 100% → 60%.** The gunboat runs alongside, 25–40 m off and bobbing. Two deck turrets (12 HP each)\n1251\t   spin up (a 0.8-s whine, which is the telegraph) and fire bursts; deckhand gunners respawn on deck, 2 at a time.\n1252\t   Fuel drums on deck (3) do heavy damage to the turret next to them. Destroying both turrets moves to phase 2.\n1253\t2. **Barracuda on the bridge wing, 60% → 25%.** She fires harpoons (projectiles, 2 HP) and **torpedoes**: a visible\n1254\t   wake, \"SHOOT THE TORPEDO!\", 2 HP, and cover doesn't help. She ducks behind the armored bridge between volleys and is\n1255\t   open for about 2 s. Her HP is 30.\n1256\t3. **Boarding, 25% → 0.** The Stingray burns. She swings across on a rope with her `cutlass`, lands on our boat and\n1257\t   slashes (a 0.6-s telegraph; cover blocks it). Each time she staggers she is open for 1.5 s, three cycles in all.\n1258\t   Defeat: \"This isn't over, CREST!\", she splashes into the sea, and the Stingray explodes in slow motion.\n1259\t\n1260\t**HAMMERHEAD** (2-B, 120 s; 60 HP, × the boss slider):\n1261\t- **A. Shield advance.** He walks behind his hatch-door shield (immune from the front); only an exposed shin takes leg\n1262\t  damage. A shotgun or GL hit knocks the shield aside for 1.5 s and opens his torso.\n1263\t- **B. Rotary sweep.** He plants the shield, the cannon spins up for 1.2 s (a whine, the barrels glowing red, a big\n1264\t  ring), then he sweeps left to right for 2.5 s; you are hit if you're exposed at any moment of it. Each sweep damages\n1265\t  your cover (intact → cracked → broken, with debris). When it breaks, the camera dives to the second cover, the lens\n1266\t  housing (\"Cover's gone—moving!\").\n1267\t- **C. Grenade volley.** He throws 3 grenades in arcs. A grenade shot down near him damages him (4 each).\n1268\t- **Below 50%.** The shield is broken and falls off the gallery (\"MY DOOR! YOU BROKE MY DOOR!\"). He **charges** like a\n1269\t  rusher; 6 damage within 1.5 s staggers him, otherwise he slams your cover. The lighthouse beam sweeps by and dazzles\n1270\t  for 0.4 s (a white flash, following the comfort settings).\n1271\t- **Defeat**: he staggers through the railing and falls into the surf below (\"HAMMERHEAD… FALLS… DOWN…\").\n1272\t\n1273\t**SCYLLA** (3-B, phase 1, 90 s):\n1274\t- A six-legged crab walker: a 3-segment IK leg rig of meshes, two claws with cannons, two missile pods, an armored\n1275\t  cockpit.\n1276\t- Six **knee joints** (8 HP each, glowing weak points).\n1277\t- Claw-cannon shots are threats (0.9-s charge glow).\n1278\t- **Missile volleys** of 6 in arcs: shoot them down, or take cover; missiles that hit your cover shake it.\n1279\t- Destroying any 3 knees **topples it** (it falls on its side): the cockpit hatch opens for 6 s (20 HP). Then it rights"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14efaf47-c831-41be-b2ba-0398106a097f","uuid":"7117ee27-61a2-45b3-8cc7-9b1f663851c9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:32:30.192Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-183213__xf5fb84/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **SFX** are registered with `audio.define`. Each weapon is a list of voices: a noise burst with a falling filter plus\n  a sine thump; the explosion is 1.2 s of decaying noise plus a 50-Hz sub. Long or filtered sounds (rain, surf, the\n  crowd, the turbine hum, sluices) are small Web Audio graphs on `audio.bus('sfx')`.\n- **Spatial mix**: pan by screen x, volume `1/(1 + d/240)`. At most 24 voices at once; quieter sounds are dropped first.\n- **Ambience per stage**:\n  - festival: crowd murmur (filtered noise), gibberish cheers, fireworks pops, gulls;\n  - storm: rain, gusts, thunder after each lightning flash, surf on the rocks;\n  - Tidewall: sluice roar, turbine hum, PA announcements in gibberish.\n- **The voice synth** (Animalese/Simlish style, `41-voice.js`):\n  - the text splits into syllables (consonant clusters plus a vowel); each syllable lasts 50–90 ms;\n  - **vowels** drive a sawtooth or pulse source through two parallel band-pass filters at the formants:\n    a 800/1200, e 400/2000, i 300/2300, o 450/800, u 325/700, y 300/1900 Hz;\n  - **consonants** add a short filtered noise burst by class: plosives (p t k b d g) 12 ms clicks; fricatives\n    (s f h z x c) 40 ms hiss; nasals (m n) a 30 ms hum; liquids (l r w) a glide into the vowel;\n  - pitch is the speaker's base × the sentence contour (falling at a \".\", rising at a \"?\", +25% and louder for\n    ALL CAPS or \"!\") × a jitter per letter (a hash ±6%);\n  - pauses: 120 ms at commas, 250 ms at sentence ends;\n  - a per-speaker effect: `radio` (a 300–3000 Hz band and crackle), `boom` (low and wide, with a short delay),\n    `gravel` (40 Hz amplitude modulation, slight drive);\n  - the same text always sounds the same (seeded by its hash).\n  - Barks (\"Hraa!\", \"Reloading!\", \"Ugh!\") use pools of short random syllable strings with no subtitles.\n  - At most 2 dialog voices and 4 barks at once.\n  - Headless (no `audio.ctx`), it schedules nothing and returns the line's duration, which the tests read.\n\n### 4.13 HUD (canvas pixel UI; anchor to `r.W`/`r.H`; keep clear of `game.cornerArea()`)\n- **Top center: TIME.**\n  - \"TIME\" over **`45.00`** (font scale 3), on a 90×28 plate whose water level rises as TIME runs out;\n  - red below 10 s, blinking below 5 s, a tick sound each second;\n  - the boss bar goes under it.\n- **Top left: SCORE** (scale 2), with the chain \"×1.4\" under it.\n- **Bottom left: LIFE**, a row of CREST shield-badge sprites that flash and break when lost.\n- **Bottom right: the weapon panel.**\n  - icon, name and ammo: 9 bullet pips for the handgun; a number and a bar for specials;\n  - a reload ring while in cover;\n  - a 4-slot strip shows for 0.8 s after a switch.\n- **Center**:\n  - the reticle in the player's color with a white outline (styles: cross, ring, dot; sizes S/M/L);\n  - a hit marker (a white ×, gold for a headshot);\n  - \"RELOAD!\" under the ammo and flashing over the reticle when empty;\n  - **\"ACTION!\"** (scale 4, slides in, 0.9 s) and **\"WAIT\"** (scale 3, blinking);\n  - \"TIME EXTEND +2\", \"TIDE'S IN!\", \"STAGE CLEAR\";\n  - score popups (\"+1500\") at the projected enemy positions;\n  - pickup banners (\"MACHINE GUN +60\");\n  - \"NEW WEAPON\" cards the first time a weapon is picked up.\n- **Threat rings and edge arrows**, as in §4.7.\n- **Cover feedback**: a dark vignette, a small \"COVER\" tag at the bottom center, the reticle hidden.\n- **Radio box**: at the top, with a 32-px live portrait, the name in the speaker's color, two lines of at most 40\n  characters, auto-advancing.\n- **Prompt bar** (bottom center): device-aware tutorial prompts the first time (\"HOLD [L2] TO TAKE COVER\").\n- **Co-op**:\n  - split screen: each viewport has its own HUD. Secondary text uses scale 1, but key numbers never go below scale 2.\n  - AI Hub: a teammate strip at the top right (name in the seat color, lives, a ● when in cover).\n- **10-foot sizes**: the smallest text is scale 1 (7 px of 240, about 31 px on a 1080p TV); key numbers are scale 2–3.\n  Check 16:9, 4:3 and 21:9.\n\n### 4.14 Screens, flow and the 10-foot UI\n**Rules**:\n- at most 7 items a screen, each with a one-line description under it;\n- one big focus highlight, animated as a wave;\n- d-pad, stick or arrows move the focus, and hovering the mouse moves it too; ✕, Enter or a click picks; ○, Esc or a\n  right click goes back;\n- no dense tables: the results show 6 big count-up stats, and lists are paged (L1/R1, the wheel, or arrows), never\n  scrolled.\n\nUse `E.Menu` (it already does hover, click and keys), drawn with our own frame (`E.ui.box`, gradient\n`['#10244a', '#081428']`, accent `#3aa0e8`, text `#f4f7ff`, gold `#ffd36a`, warning `#ff3b30`).\n\n```\nboot → title ─(any button)→ menu ─┬ ARCADE → difficulty → briefing(1) → play(1) → results → briefing(2) … → ending → initials → records → title\n                                  ├ CO-OP → coop join (P2 press START on a pad) → difficulty → (as ARCADE, split screen)\n                                  ├ STAGE SELECT → 3 cards → difficulty → briefing(n) → play(n) → results → initials → title\n                                  ├ TRAINING → play(range) → range results → menu\n                                  ├ RECORDS (tables per mode · range best · INTEL)   ├ SETTINGS (engine screens)\n                                  └ ABOUT (pages)                                     └ HOW TO PLAY (device-aware diagram)\nplay: pause (engine menu: Resume · Restart scene · Restart stage · How to play · Dev mode · Settings · Controls · Full screen · Quit to title)\ntitle/menu idle 10 s → attract (§4.17)          AI Hub: boot → session card → play(round stage) → session results\n```\n**The screens**:\n- **Title**: a slow flyover of Stage 1's night harbor with fireworks, rendered by Rail3D.\n  - The logo is `E.font.title` \"TIDE CRISIS\" (scale 4; colors `#ffffff #9fe3ff #3aa0e8 #1a4f9c`; outline `#08142a`;\n    depth 3) with a big \"3\" in `#fff3b0 #ffb347 #ff5a2a #a81e1e` and an animated wave band under it.\n  - \"PRESS ✕\" for a pad, \"PRESS ENTER OR CLICK\" for keyboard and mouse.\n  - `E.versionLabel()` small, at the bottom.\n- **Touch-only** devices (coarse pointer, no fine pointer, no gamepad, no key pressed yet): the title shows **\"Controller\n  or keyboard required\"** and a large gear button that opens `game.menu('settings')`. There are no touch controls\n  (`touch: []`, `input.touchEnabled = false`). The attract mode runs. A key press or a connected pad unlocks the\n  normal title.\n- **Briefing**: map pages; skip with ✕ or Enter.\n- **Results**: the stage name, then count-ups (time, accuracy, kills, headshots, cover saves, lives lost), the time\n  bonus, the accuracy bonus (`accuracy% × 300`), the no-damage bonus (20,000) and the total. The rank letter lands\n  with a shake.\n- **Game over**: \"GAME OVER\" → initials on `E.Arcade`'s letter wheel if the score qualifies → the table.\n- **Ending**: the cutscene, the credits roll (cast with live portraits, \"Made with my-3D2dge\", the music), the final\n  score, the initials.\n- **Records**: tables `arcade`, `coop`, `stage1`–`stage3` (via `E.Arcade.table/qualifies/enter/drawTable`), the range's\n  best time, and INTEL (the bottles found, each with its short funny note).\n- **About**, as pages:\n  1. the game and its credits;\n  2. **Source Information**;\n  3. **Testing Feedback**;\n  4. **Engine Feedback**;\n  5. **Prompt Feedback**;\n  6. thanks and licenses (the engine is MIT; the asset store is CC0 if used).\n\n  All of it comes from `src/99-about.js`.\n\n### 4.15 Settings (our items, added through `menu.settings` beside the engine's)\n| Key | Label | Options (default) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `difficulty` | Difficulty (new runs) | Easy / **Normal** / Hard |\n| `aimAssist` | Aim assist (controller) | Off / Low / **Medium** / High |\n| `padSpeed` | Aim speed (controller) | slider 0.5–2.0 (**1.0**) |\n| `coverMode` | Cover control | **Hold** / Toggle |\n| `autoFire` | Hold fire to repeat (handgun) | **On** / Off |\n| `reticle` / `reticleSize` | Reticle / size | **Cross** / Ring / Dot · S / **M** / L |\n| `threatSize` | Crisis rings | **Normal** / Large |\n| `subtitles` | Subtitles | **On** / Off |\n| `wobble` | Retro texture wobble | **Off** / On |\n\nThe engine already provides Button symbols (patched to PlayStation by default, with Xbox), volumes, comfort settings\n(shake and flashes, which our FX respect), screen filters (CRT, scanlines), dead zone, invert Y, vibration and\nrebinding. Its Controls screen names our actions: `menu.controls = { fire: 'Fire', cover: 'Take cover', next: 'Next weapon', prev: 'Previous weapon', reload: 'Quick reload', w1: 'Handgun', … }`.\n\n### 4.16 Dev mode (from the pause menu; not available in AI Hub sessions)\n\"Dev mode\" in the pause menu resumes the engine and opens our **canvas dev panel**. The Sim is frozen while the panel\nis open; close it to play with the new values. It has 6 tabs (L1/R1, Q/E or a click):\n\n1. **PLAYER**: God mode · Infinite ammo · Start lives (1–9, 5) · I-frames (0.5–3 s, 1.5) · Cover in (0.05–0.4, 0.12) ·\n   Cover out (0.05–0.4, 0.15) · Reload (0.1–1.0, 0.30) · Pad aim speed (0.5–3, 1.6) · Aim assist (0–1, .6).\n2. **WEAPONS**: handgun damage (0.5–3, 1) · MG rate (4–20/s, 12) · MG damage (0.1–1, .45) · shotgun pellets (4–14, 8) ·\n   shotgun spread (2–12°, 5.5) · GL radius (24–96, 48) · GL damage (2–12, 6) · Ammo pickups × (0.5–3, 1).\n3. **ENEMIES**: HP × (0.5–3, 1) · Threat chance × (0–3, 1) · Telegraph × (0.5–2, 1) · Fire interval × (0.5–2, 1) ·\n   Max threats (1–4) · Minimum gap (0.1–1 s, .35) · Reaction delay × (0.5–2, 1) · Boss HP × (0.25–3, 1).\n4. **TIME**: Freeze timer · Scene time × (0.5–2, 1) · Elite bonus (0–5 s, 2) · Refill after time-up (20–100%, 60) ·\n   Game speed (0.25–1.5, 1).\n5. **VIEW**: FOV (45–75, 55) · Show hit volumes · Colliders · Rail and cover points · AI state labels · Threat debug ·\n   Perf overlay (ms per pass, triangles, pixels, actors) · Free camera · Hide HUD.\n6. **ACTIONS**: Restart scene · Next / previous scene · Jump to… (every scene, boss and cutscene) · Kill all · Spawn\n   enemy (pick a type; it appears at the nearest free cover point) · All weapons and full ammo · +1 life · Lose a life ·\n   Trigger a threat on me · Clear stage · Game over · Bot plays (Autopilot) · Add a P2 bot (split-screen test) ·\n   Voice test (each speaker says a line) · Music test (every song and layer).\n\n**RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits at the bottom, with a confirmation. □ or Backspace resets the focused item.\n- Changed values show in gold, with the default in grey.\n- Navigation: up/down move; left/right adjust (holding accelerates); ✕ or Enter toggles or runs; ○ or Esc closes.\n  Hovering the mouse focuses, dragging moves a slider's knob, and the wheel adjusts.\n- Values live in `tune` for the visit, and Reset copies `TUNE` back.\n- Every slider's key is a path into `tune` (`'enemy.hpMul'`), and the panel is generated from a descriptor list, so\n  adding a variable takes one line.\n\n### 4.17 Attract mode and the Autopilot\n**The attract controller** (`53-attract.js`):\n- **Trigger**: 10 s on the title or the main menu with no input. Input means any key or button, a pointer move of more\n  than 4 px, the wheel, or a stick past its dead zone.\n- **The cycle**: DEMO (highlight *i*, about 28 s) → HIGH SCORES (6 s) → DEMO (*i+1*) → TITLE (8 s) → repeat, carrying\n  on through the playlist.\n- **The playlist** (`def('highlight', …)`), in order:\n  1. 1-1 Lantern Pier\n  2. 1-4 Canal Run\n  3. 1-B Barracuda\n  4. 2-1 Gull's Rest\n  5. 2-3 Funicular and The Gull\n  6. 2-B Hammerhead\n  7. 3-2 Sluice Locks and Seawolf\n  8. 3-3 Turbine Hall\n  9. 3-B Scylla and Krake\n- **The demo** is `Sim({ mode: 'attract' })` jumped to the highlight. It cheats freely: god mode, infinite ammo, travel\n  ×1.5. Telegraphs still show, so the mechanic is visible. The bot plays in `showoff` style.\n- The overlay shows \"DEMO PLAY\", the small logo and a blinking \"PRESS START\" in the right device label. The HUD stays.\n- **Any input** goes back to the title, and that input is consumed so it doesn't also pick a menu item.\n- **Watchdog**: if nothing progresses for 8 s, move to the next highlight.\n- On touch-only devices the attract mode runs the same way; a tap goes back to the title, which shows the notice.\n\n**The Autopilot** produces the same `Command` objects a player does. It drives tests (`fair`), the attract mode\n(`showoff`) and the dev \"P2 bot\".\n- **Perception**: it reads the Sim state, including threats and their timers.\n- **Target priority**:\n  1. threats on me, soonest impact first;\n  2. projectiles in flight;\n  3. Red Tide and snipers;\n  4. rushers, nearest first;\n  5. shields, if a shotgun or GL is available;\n  6. the rest, by exposure time left.\n\n  Never civilians, hostages or allies. When idle: life rings, bottles, and barrels next to enemies.\n- **Aim**: a critically damped spring toward the target's head (headshot chance by skill) or chest, with a maximum\n  speed and a reaction delay. It fires when the projected reticle is inside the target's volume, with jitter by skill.\n- **Cover**: hide when a threat on me has under 0.25 s left and its source won't die first; hide to reload when empty\n  (or at ≤ 2 rounds with no threats); pop out when no impact is due within 0.3 s.\n- **Weapons**: MG against drones and groups; the shotgun against rushers, shields and close range; the GL against\n  vehicles, barricades and bosses' armored parts; otherwise the handgun.\n- **Skills**:\n\n| Skill | Reaction | Speed | Headshots |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `fair` | 0.30 s | 2.2 vu/s | 30% |\n| `expert` | 0.18 s | 3.5 vu/s | 60% |\n| `showoff` | `expert`, and uses every weapon and bonus target | | |\n\n### 4.18 Local co-op (split screen)\n- **CO-OP** opens a join screen: \"PLAYER 2 — PRESS START ON A CONTROLLER\".\n  - `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`; the new player is `game.addPlayer({ pad: i })`.\n  - P1 keeps the keyboard and mouse and any other pad. The mouse always belongs to P1.\n  - In ARCADE, a second pad's Start also drops P2 in during play, with a slide into split screen.\n- `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'columns' })`. Rail3D renders each viewport with that player's camera, and the HUD is\n  drawn per viewport.\n- One Sim with two players: shared enemies, time and rail; separate slots, cover, aim, weapons, ammo, lives, score\n  and continues.\n  - Threats choose a target player.\n  - Pickups go to the player who made the kill.\n  - Life rings go to whoever shoots them.\n- When a player is out, their half shows \"CONTINUE? 10\" while the other plays on. If both are out, it's game over.\n\n### 4.19 AI Hub online co-op (shared world; follow `lib/hub/README.md`, protocol 1)\n**Tags** in `<head>`, after the session client:\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Clear the stage together; most points wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim mouse/stick · Fire click/R2 · Hold right-click/L2: cover+reload\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"7\">\n```\nThe goal must stay at 60 characters or fewer and the howto at 80 or fewer.\n\n**Flow** when `AIHub.active`:\n1. Skip the title, the attract mode and every menu. Use `new E.Game({ autoPause: false, corner: false, fullscreen: false, … })`,\n   and make the play scene not pausable.\n2. In the first boot step, call\n   `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. If it returns `null`,\n   carry on as a normal game.\n3. Build the round's stage: **stage = ((info.round − 1) mod 3) + 1**. Seats map to agents in seat order: Kai, Rook,\n   Mari, Juno, and the colors come from `info.players`.\n4. Call `AIHub.ready()` with no key press.\n5. Show **our own card**, the session briefing: the stage name and art, every player in their color with a READY tick,\n   the goal, this device's controls, and the countdown from the `card` event.\n   - Enter or ✕ (button 0) calls `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n   - △ (button 3) or M calls `AIHub.answer('more')`; answering again takes it back.\n   - The card has no menus or choices, and draws no `Math.random`.\n6. On `start`, count down 3-2-1 while `AIHub.time() < 0`; ACTION! at 0. No intro cutscene.\n7. **Host** (`info.isHost`) runs the authoritative Sim with every player.\n   - **Server** (`AIHub.server`): no local player, input or camera. Show a status screen (\"HOSTING · 3 AGENTS ·\n     AREA 1-3\") and keep the world running.\n8. The end:\n   - the host calls `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won, stats, awards })` once: on the boss's defeat, when every\n     player has left, or immediately on `time-up`;\n   - `standings` group equal scores; `won` is true if the boss was defeated;\n   - stats per player: Kills, Accuracy, Headshots, Cover saves, Lives lost;\n   - awards: Sharpshooter (best accuracy), Pointman (most kills), Iron Wall (most cover saves);\n   - on `end`: stop input (`input.clear()`, ignore everything after), `audio.stopMusic()` and mute, and show \"ROUND OVER\"\n     (or \"HOST LEFT — ROUND VOID\", or \"YOU SAT THIS ONE OUT\").\n9. **Rules**:\n   - network only through `AIHub.send`;\n   - no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation;\n   - Quit, Restart and Title do nothing;\n   - Options while Create is held is ignored;\n   - names are only ever drawn to the canvas, never into HTML.\n\n**Messages** (short keys, quantized integers):\n\n| Direction | Message | Reliability, rate | Content |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| client → host | `in` | unreliable, 15/s | `{ t: 'in', at, a: [ax·1000, ay·1000], c: 0/1, w }` |\n| client → host | `sh` | reliable, ≤ 8/s, only when something happened | shots `[[at, w, ax, ay], …]` and cover changes `[[at, c], …]` since the last |\n| host → all | `s` | unreliable, 10/s, **≤ 1,100 bytes** | `{ t: 's', at, ph, sc, d, tm, p: [[id, score, lives, chain, w, ammo, c]], e: [[id, type, x, y, z, yaw, state, hp]], pr: [[id, kind, x, y, z]], b: [phase, …hp] }` |\n| host → all | `ev` | reliable, ≤ 8/s, batched | spawn, kill (with the shooter), hit, threat (`{ id, src, target, t0, dur }` in hub time), pickup, cue, scene, clear, over |\n\n- **Rate limit**: a token bucket of 18 messages per second per copy (the protocol caps it at 20). Skip a snapshot when\n  the bucket is empty; reliable batches merge.\n- **The host**:\n  - applies each player's latest `in` and every `sh` shot, rewinding the enemies: it keeps a 300-ms ring buffer of hit\n    volumes at 30 Hz and tests each shot against the sample nearest its `at`, clamped 0–250 ms into the past;\n  - keeps a cover timeline per player;\n  - resolves each threat at T + 0.2 s against the cover state that player reported at T.\n- **The replica** (a client):\n  - renders enemies interpolated at `AIHub.time() − 120 ms`, driving rigs from position, velocity and state codes;\n  - draws threat rings from `threat` events on the hub's timeline;\n  - predicts its own cover, weapon and ammo at once and corrects them when the snapshot disagrees for 0.5 s;\n  - takes score and lives from the host;\n  - shows its own shots' sparks and hit markers at once, against the interpolated volumes, as cosmetics only; the\n    host's `kill` events decide.\n- **When a player leaves**: their agent disappears and threats retarget. If the host leaves, the clients get\n  `end: host-left`.\n- A snapshot over budget drops the enemies furthest from every camera; they come round-robin in the next snapshot.\n\n### 4.20 Records, saves and intel\n- High-score tables go through `E.Arcade` (modes `arcade`, `coop`, `stage1`–`stage3`). Don't use `E.Arcade.add`: it\n  floats points in 2D world space. Use only `table`, `qualifies`, `enter` and `drawTable`.\n- The range's best time, the flags (`seenTraining`, `seenPrompts.*`) and the INTEL bottles found (6: two per stage) go\n  in `E.store`.\n- There are no mid-run saves; this is an arcade game. STAGE SELECT makes every stage reachable.\n- In AI Hub sessions, storage is the client's memory-only storage, so nothing persists. That's fine.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Content\n\n### 5.1 World and cast\nThe **Lumen Isles** are an island republic whose capital, **Port Lumen**, lies below sea level behind the **Meridian\nTidewall**, a 3-km tidal barrier and power plant. Tonight is the **Lantern Festival**, on the eve of the **king tide**,\nthe highest in eighteen years, which peaks at **06:00**. **Admiral Silas Krake**, court-martialed after a storm\ndisaster he blames on the city, leads the **Black Current** mercenary fleet. He has seized the harbor and the\nTidewall, and at the peak he will open all forty floodgates.\n\n| Character | Role | Look (Humanoid options) | Voice |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Kai Navarro** (P1, seat 1, red) | CREST agent; calm, fond of one-liners | `heroic`, size 1.05; charcoal suit coat, red tie (trim), short black hair; `gun` | 150 Hz, saw, 11 syllables/s |\n| **Rook Halvorsen** (P2, seat 2, blue) | CREST demolitions, ex-navy diver; gruff; calls Kai \"kid\" | `bulky`, 1.1; brown leather coat, blue shirt, bald | 105 Hz, `gravel`, 9/s |\n| **Mari Tejada** (P3, seat 3, green) | Lieutenant, Lumen Coast Guard; drives the boats | `heroic`, 1.0; orange and white coast-guard jacket with green trim, ponytail | 230 Hz, bright, 12/s |\n| **Juno Achterberg** (P4, seat 4, yellow) | CREST tech on the radio (in hub sessions, a field agent) | `chibi`, 1.0; yellow headset (band hat), spiky teal hair | 200 Hz, nasal, `radio`, 13/s |\n| **Director Ines Hale** | CREST HQ; briefings | `heroic`, 1.0; grey coat, long silver hair | 175 Hz, `radio`, 10/s |\n| **Dr. Tomas Tejada** | Tidewall chief engineer; a hostage | white lab coat, short grey hair | 140 Hz, warm, 9/s |\n| **Admiral Silas Krake** | Villain; the final boss | `heroic`, 1.15; long navy greatcoat with gold trim, cape, long white hair, naval cap; `cutlass` + `flare` | 95 Hz, `boom`, 8/s |\n| **Brigitte \"Barracuda\" Sorel** | Stage 1 boss, captain of the gunboat *Stingray* | teal wetsuit with armor, white ponytail; `harpoon`, then `cutlass` | 210 Hz, staccato, 13/s |\n| **Bruno \"Hammerhead\" Kessler** | Stage 2 boss, the enforcer | `bulky`, 1.6; armor, flat-topped steel helmet; `minigun` + `hatch` shield | 80 Hz, `gravel`, 6/s, often in CAPS |\n| **The Gull** | Stage 2 sniper mini-boss | a hooded grey coat; `sniper` with a laser | 190 Hz, whispery |\n\nThe vehicles and machines are the **Stingray** gunboat, the **Bulldog** armored truck, the **Seawolf** gunship\nhelicopter and **Scylla**, a six-legged crab walker.\n\n### 5.2 Tone guide (the source's B-movie style; original lines)\n- Short, punchy, earnest. Names get said a lot, and exclamation marks are common. At most 70 characters a page, and at\n  most two lines.\n- Heroes trade cool one-liners and their partner groans at them. HQ talks in urgent radio jargon (\"Copy that\",\n  \"HQ to Kai\").\n- The villain is grandiose and speaks in sea metaphors. Henchmen announce themselves.\n- Keep it slightly over the top, as 2000s arcade localization was, but never broken English, and never mocking.\n\n### 5.3 Enemy roster\n| id | Look | HP | Entries | Peek | Exposed for | Fire | Threat chance / telegraph | Score | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `deckhand` | navy sweater, watch cap, `rifle`; 4 skin tones | 1 | stand, run, jump, door | up, side | 1.4–2.2 s | burst of 3 at 0.12 s | 25% (S1) → 40% (S3) / 1.10 s | 1,000 | the common grunt |\n| `marine` | olive armor, helmet, `rifle` | 2 | run, rappel, vault | up, side | 1.6–2.4 | burst of 4 | 35% / 0.95 | 1,500 | flinches on the first hit |\n| `redtide` | crimson coat, beret, balaclava, `smg` | 2 | roll, jump | side | 0.9–1.3 | burst of 2 | **100% / 0.55** | 3,000, +2 s | radio warns the first time |\n| `quartermaster` | **orange life vest**, crate on the back (`offhand: 'crate'`) | 1 | runs a route across | — | 2.5–4 s crossing | rarely: 10% / 1.2 | — | 2,000 | drops the scene's ammo; flees |\n| `grenadier` | `bulky`, bandolier | 2 | stand, run | up | 1.2 | lobs a grenade | the grenade lands as a threat | 2,000 | the grenade can be shot down |\n| `rocketeer` | helmet, goggles, `launcher` | 2 | stand (high) | up, side | 2.0 aim | a rocket | 100% (aim glint 0.8 + flight) | 2,500 | the rocket can be shot down |\n| `shield` | `bulky`, riot shield, `smg` | 3 | advances between covers | over | — | burst of 3 | 40% / 0.9 | 3,000 | immune from the front; a shotgun or GL hit drops the shield aside for 1.5 s; head open when he fires |\n| `rusher` | tank top, bandana, `cutlass` | 1.5 | sprints in a zigzag | — | 2–3 s approach | slashes at 2 m | 100% / 0.45 wind-up | 2,000 | cover blocks the slash; he staggers back and runs in again |\n| `sniper` | hooded, `sniper` | 1 | stand (far, high) | up | long | 1 shot | 100% / 1.4 (the laser converges) | 3,000, +2 s | the beam is visible in the world |\n| `drone` | a small quadrotor mesh with a red eye light | 2 | flies a path | — | erratic | zap beam | 30% / 0.9 | 1,500 | falls in sparks |\n| `jetski` | a rider on a jet ski | rider 1 / ski 3 | races a water path | — | a pass | pistol | 30% / 1.0 | 1,500 (+1,000 for the ski) | the ski's explosion kills the rider |\n| `gunner` | a mounted gun (bridge or truck) | 3 | stand | — | — | long bursts | 50% / 1.0 | 2,500 | |\n| `captor` | Red Tide holding a hostage | 1 (head only) | stand | side (head) | 0.7 s | pistol | 100% / 0.8 | 5,000 | hitting the hostage = −1 life |\n| `civilian` | festival-goers, dock workers, fishermen | 1 | run, hide | — | — | — | — | −5,000 | panics and crouches |\n\n### 5.4 Stage 1, HARBOR LIGHTS (Port Lumen Docks, 23:40, Lantern Festival)\n**Look**:\n- night blue; the sky goes `#0b1026 → #1b2a52 → #3a3060` with a magenta city glow at the horizon; fog `#1a2242`\n  400–2000;\n- warm paper lanterns (`#ffb347`, static lights of radius 90);\n- festival string lights (emissive `#ff5a5a #ffd34a #5ad1ff #8aff8a`);\n- fireworks bursting over the bay, each flash a dynamic light;\n- dark water with lantern sparkles.\n\nMusic: \"Harbor Lights\".\n\n| Scene | Time | Setting and cover | Waves (about 8–14 enemies) | New in this scene | Objects and cues |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1-0 intro** | — | Mari's speedboat races in under fireworks; Krake's broadcast plays on the festival's giant screens (a live portrait texture); they dock at Lantern Pier | — | — | lines in §5.9 |\n| **1-1 LANTERN PIER** | 45 | A 7.5-m wooden pier lined with lantern stalls; the warehouses of the quay ahead (x 380). P1 hides behind stacked fish crates, P2 behind barrels and a bollard (the schema example in §4.10) | (a) 3 deckhands pop up behind stalls; (b) one comes out of the warehouse door, one jumps off a fishing boat's cabin roof; (c) a quartermaster crosses the quay (MG +60) and a deckhand runs in | cover, reload, the handgun, the MG pickup, civilians | a festival couple flees early (don't shoot); breakable lanterns and bottles; **life ring** on a post; first-time prompts for cover and reload |\n| **1-2 FISH MARKET** | 50 | Travel: along the quay, then Rook kicks in a side door. Inside, a brick hall with a tiled floor, wooden stalls, ice tables, hanging paper lanterns and an upper gallery | (a) 4 deckhands, 2 of them on the gallery, who fall when killed; (b) **the first Red Tide** rolls out from behind the ice table; (c) 2 marines vault the stalls, and a grenadier on the gallery throws the **first grenade** | Red Tide, grenades, explosive red generator tanks under the gallery (shooting them drops a section of it, killing 2) | an **intel bottle** on a shelf; Rook visible fighting at the far end |\n| **1-3 CONTAINER YARD** | 60 | Berth 9: container stacks (red, blue, ochre, green), a gantry crane, sodium lamps, forklifts | (a) 3 deckhands jump down off containers and 2 hide behind forklifts; (b) **BULLDOG**, an armored truck, smashes through a container wall: its gunner (3 HP, above the gun shield, 50% threat), then its fuel tank (6 HP) for a huge blast; 2 marines climb out of the back; (c) a grenadier and a quartermaster (MG) | a vehicle set piece | a dock worker hides behind a forklift (\"Don't shoot! I just work here!\") |\n| **1-4 CANAL RUN** (moving) | 60 | Mari drives through the Silver Canals under festival bridges; duck behind the boat's armored gunwale (part of the carrier); spray, sway | 3 pairs of jet skis from side canals; bridge gunners on 2 bridges, with deckhands on the railings who fall into the water; a quartermaster on a moored boat | a moving fight, jet skis | a fireworks barge: shoot its crates and the rockets wreck an enemy boat; a low bridge (\"Heads down!\", purely visual) |\n| **1-B BOSS: STINGRAY → BARRACUDA** | 100 | The harbor mouth, the two boats side by side; cover behind the gunwale | §5.7 | the boss framework, torpedoes | — |\n| **1-Z outro** | — | Barracuda falls into the sea; the Stingray explodes; Juno traces Krake's signal | — | — | STAGE CLEAR → results |\n\n### 5.5 Stage 2, STORM SIGNAL (Gull's Rest and Lighthouse Point, 02:10, thunderstorm)\n**Look**:\n- slate and green-grey, with heavy rain (screen streaks and splashes);\n- lightning every 9–15 s: the sky flashes, a white `game.flash` at 0.25, thunder after 0.5–1.5 s;\n- fog `#26343c` 200–1200;\n- timber and plaster houses with slate roofs; wet stone (a `wet` overlay pattern that brightens lit bands);\n- fishermen in yellow raincoats;\n- the lighthouse beam sweeps every 8 s: a visible cone of glowing lines and a dynamic light.\n\nMusic: \"Storm Signal\".\n\n| Scene | Time | Setting and cover | Waves | New | Objects and cues |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **2-0 intro** | — | The boat docks in the rain; lightning shows the lighthouse; Krake's radio intercept and Hammerhead's reply | — | — | — |\n| **2-1 GULL'S REST** | 50 | A steep cobbled lane, timber houses, washing lines, boats up on blocks; cover behind a fish cart and a stone well | (a) deckhands in windows (the glass breaks); (b) **rushers**, 2 then 3, sprinting down the lane; (c) a quartermaster from an alley drops the **SHOTGUN +8** (\"NEW WEAPON\"); marines behind a cart barricade | rushers, the shotgun | a fisherman ducks in a doorway; laundry that tears and swings |\n| **2-2 NET LOFTS** | 55 | Inside a tall wooden loft: stairs, hanging nets (`alphaTest`), fish-oil barrels | (a) a **shield** trooper advances down the stairs (\"Shotgun! Knock that shield aside!\"); (b) 3 **rappellers** drop through a roof hatch; (c) 2 Red Tide and a grenadier on the upper floor | shields, rappelling | the fish-oil barrels explode into fire; an **intel bottle** |\n| **2-3 FUNICULAR** (moving) | 60 | The cliff funicular climbs through the storm; duck behind the car's bench and bulkhead | deckhands on the cliff-path terraces; **the passing car**: the descending car crosses with 3 gunners in a 4-s window; at the top station the car stops for **THE GULL**: a laser sniper among 3 perches on the lighthouse gallery, 4 HP (a headshot does 2) | snipers and laser telegraphs; a mini-boss | a quartermaster at the top station (MG); the **life ring** |\n| **2-4 LIGHTHOUSE KEEP** | 60 | The courtyard, the keeper's house, the lighthouse door; then up the **spiral stairs** (vertical travel; shoot upward) | (a) marines and a Red Tide; (b) **hostage**: a captor holds Dr. Tejada at the door (\"One more step and the old man swims!\"); his head shows for 0.7-s windows; (c) rushers and deckhands through the house windows; 2 deckhands on the stairs above | hostages | rescuing Dr. Tejada is a cue |\n| **2-B BOSS: HAMMERHEAD** | 120 | The lantern gallery at the top: the giant rotating lens, the storm, cover in the doorway and then behind the lens housing | §5.7 | cover that degrades and a scripted cover swap | — |\n| **2-Z outro** | — | Dr. Tejada reveals the override is in the control tower; Juno: take the maintenance tram | — | — | results |\n\n### 5.6 Stage 3, THE TIDEWALL (05:20 → 06:00, dawn)\n**Look**:\n- dawn: violet, then orange; the sky goes `#1f1a3a → #6a3f6f → #f29e6b` and the sun rises over the stage; fog\n  `#8a7a8f` 500–2400;\n- concrete slab, steel, hazard stripes (`#f2c12e`/`#1b1b1b`), sodium lamps (`#ffb04a`);\n- teal water that **rises** through the stage: the water z goes up 1.5 u per scene and 4 u in the final fight;\n- white foam at the sluices; the turbine hall's emissive screens (`#43f0c8`).\n\nMusic: \"Tidewall\".\n\n| Scene | Time | Setting and cover | Waves | New | Objects and cues |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **3-0 intro** | — | The maintenance tram runs along the crest, the sea high on one side and the city low on the other; Krake on the barrier's giant screens | — | — | — |\n| **3-1 CREST ROAD** | 50 | The crest road, gate-machinery houses, lamp posts, a barricade | (a) a swarm of 5 **drones** (\"Use the machine gun!\"); (b) 2 **rocketeers** on gantries (\"Rocket! Shoot it down!\"); (c) a quartermaster drops the **GRENADE LAUNCHER +2**; marines and a shield behind a barricade (GL blasts it apart) | drones, rockets, the GL | — |\n| **3-2 SLUICE LOCKS** | 70 | Catwalks over the roaring sluices; the giant gate | **4 glowing gate locks**, 6 HP each, to jam before the tide gauge peaks (a scene-local gauge); **SEAWOLF**, a gunship helicopter, strafes (threat bursts), drops rappellers and retreats after 30 damage; deckhands and Red Tide on the catwalks; drones | an objective set piece | Dr. Tejada on the radio guides you |\n| **3-3 TURBINE HALL** | 60 | An interior: spinning turbines (dynamic meshes), steam vents, cranes, catwalks | (a) marines and Red Tide on catwalks; (b) 2 shields and rushers; (c) a grenadier and a rocketeer | combinations of everything | coolant tanks (explosive, white vapor); an **intel bottle**; Rook across the hall |\n| **3-4 TOWER LIFT** (moving) | 50 | The glass elevator on the outside of the control tower; duck behind the lift's console | drones; rappellers on the lift roof; **SEAWOLF returns**: 2 weapon pods (10 HP each), then the rotor hub (15 HP), and it crashes into the sea | — | the **life ring** on the lift |\n| **3-B FINAL: SCYLLA → KRAKE** | 90 + 90 | The tower roof and helipad (behind a concrete parapet), then the control room (behind the consoles) | §5.7 | the **FINAL SHOT** | — |\n| **3-Z ending** | — | The gates close, the sunrise floods in, Krake is arrested, then the credits | — | — | the ending and the credits roll |\n\n### 5.7 Bosses (patterns)\n**STINGRAY → BARRACUDA** (1-B, 100 s):\n1. **The Stingray, 100% → 60%.** The gunboat runs alongside, 25–40 m off and bobbing. Two deck turrets (12 HP each)\n   spin up (a 0.8-s whine, which is the telegraph) and fire bursts; deckhand gunners respawn on deck, 2 at a time.\n   Fuel drums on deck (3) do heavy damage to the turret next to them. Destroying both turrets moves to phase 2.\n2. **Barracuda on the bridge wing, 60% → 25%.** She fires harpoons (projectiles, 2 HP) and **torpedoes**: a visible\n   wake, \"SHOOT THE TORPEDO!\", 2 HP, and cover doesn't help. She ducks behind the armored bridge between volleys and is\n   open for about 2 s. Her HP is 30.\n3. **Boarding, 25% → 0.** The Stingray burns. She swings across on a rope with her `cutlass`, lands on our boat and\n   slashes (a 0.6-s telegraph; cover blocks it). Each time she staggers she is open for 1.5 s, three cycles in all.\n   Defeat: \"This isn't over, CREST!\", she splashes into the sea, and the Stingray explodes in slow motion.\n\n**HAMMERHEAD** (2-B, 120 s; 60 HP, × the boss slider):\n- **A. Shield advance.** He walks behind his hatch-door shield (immune from the front); only an exposed shin takes leg\n  damage. A shotgun or GL hit knocks the shield aside for 1.5 s and opens his torso.\n- **B. Rotary sweep.** He plants the shield, the cannon spins up for 1.2 s (a whine, the barrels glowing red, a big\n  ring), then he sweeps left to right for 2.5 s; you are hit if you're exposed at any moment of it. Each sweep damages\n  your cover (intact → cracked → broken, with debris). When it breaks, the camera dives to the second cover, the lens\n  housing (\"Cover's gone—moving!\").\n- **C. Grenade volley.** He throws 3 grenades in arcs. A grenade shot down near him damages him (4 each).\n- **Below 50%.** The shield is broken and falls off the gallery (\"MY DOOR! YOU BROKE MY DOOR!\"). He **charges** like a\n  rusher; 6 damage within 1.5 s staggers him, otherwise he slams your cover. The lighthouse beam sweeps by and dazzles\n  for 0.4 s (a white flash, following the comfort settings).\n- **Defeat**: he staggers through the railing and falls into the surf below (\"HAMMERHEAD… FALLS… DOWN…\").\n\n**SCYLLA** (3-B, phase 1, 90 s):\n- A six-legged crab walker: a 3-segment IK leg rig of meshes, two claws with cannons, two missile pods, an armored\n  cockpit.\n- Six **knee joints** (8 HP each, glowing weak points).\n- Claw-cannon shots are threats (0.9-s charge glow).\n- **Missile volleys** of 6 in arcs: shoot them down, or take cover; missiles that hit your cover shake it.\n- Destroying any 3 knees **topples it** (it falls on its side): the cockpit hatch opens for 6 s (20 HP). Then it rights","numLines":440,"startLine":840,"totalLines":1671}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ECidTbyz27hY1tFmadn94D","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
