ssistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2baVCqbK9gysobS4ep\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":140},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1181,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":467983,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":1181},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"993c4a96-27a8-44f5-b5c9-a19d46e1e96e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:34:38.936Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2baDpSG2jjme8YuQa8\",\"wire_tool_inputs\":{\"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":140}}}"," {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t# SKILLER7\\n2\\t\\n3\\t*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\\n4\\t\\n5\\t**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \\\"Killer7\\\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\\n6\\twith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\\n7\\tways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\\n8\\t`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\\n9\\t\\n10\\t**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\\n11\\tfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\\n12\\tfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\\n13\\tthem \\\"guests\\\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\\n14\\ton rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\\n15\\tThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\\n16\\tbefore they reach you.\\n17\\t\\n18\\t---\\n19\\t\\n20\\t## 0. How to use this plan\\n21\\t\\n22\\t- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\\n23\\t  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\\n24\\t- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\\n25\\t  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\\n26\\t  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\\n27\\t  art, music, levels or look.\\n28\\t- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\\n29\\t  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\\n30\\t  piece of content in this plan.\\n31\\t- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\\n32\\t  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\\n33\\t\\n34\\t---\\n35\\t\\n36\\t## 1. At a glance\\n37\\t\\n38\\t| | |\\n39\\t|---|---|\\n40\\t| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\\n41\\t| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\\n42\\t| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\\n43\\t| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\\n44\\t| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\\n45\\t| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\\n46\\t| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \\\"Turn-Down Service\\\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \\\"Après\\\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\\n47\\t| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\\n48\\t| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\\n49\\t| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\\n50\\t| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\\n51\\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\\n52\\t| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\\n53\\t\\n54\\t---\\n55\\t\\n56\\t## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\\n57\\t\\n58\\t| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\\n59\\t|---|---|\\n60\\t| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\\n61\\t| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\\\"THAW.\\\") |\\n62\\t| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\\n63\\t| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\\n64\\t| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\\n65\\t| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\\n66\\t| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \\\"Gratuity is customary.\\\") |\\n67\\t| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\\n68\\t| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\\n69\\t| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\\n70\\t\\n71\\t---\\n72\\t\\n73\\t## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\\n74\\t\\n75\\t**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\\n76\\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\\n77\\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\\n78\\tnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\\n79\\t\\n80\\t**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\\n81\\t- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\\n82\\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\\n83\\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\\n84\\t  (its `<title>` starts with \\\"Skiller7\\\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\\n85\\t  holds a port.\\n86\\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\\n87\\t  the dev folder.\\n88\\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n89\\t- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\\n90\\t  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\\n91\\t\\n92\\t---\\n93\\t\\n94\\t## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\\n95\\t\\n96\\t**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\\n97\\t- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\\n98\\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\\n99\\t- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\\n100\\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\\n101\\t\\n102\\t`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\\n103\\t\\n104\\t| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\\n105\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n106\\t| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\\n107\\t| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\\n108\\t| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\\n109\\t| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\\n110\\t| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\\n111\\t| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\\n112\\t| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\\n113\\t| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\\n114\\t\\n115\\t**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\\n116\\t```json\\n117\\t{ \\\"imports\\\": {\\n118\\t  \\\"three\\\":        \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\\\",\\n119\\t  \\\"three/webgpu\\\": \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\\\",\\n120\\t  \\\"three/tsl\\\":    \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\\\",\\n121\\t  \\\"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\\\": \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\\\" } }\\n122\\t```\\n123\\t`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\\n124\\tnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\\n125\\tdouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\\n126\\tand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\\n127\\t\\n128\\t**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\\n129\\thandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\\n130\\tconcatenates (§5.8).\\n131\\t\\n132\\t**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\\n133\\tfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\\n134\\twe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\\n135\\tlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\\n136\\tengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\\n137\\tsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\\n138\\t\\n139\\t---\\n140\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"f3959bb6-ab30-421f-8f37-05c8240fa8ea\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:34:38.943Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md\",\"content\":\"# SKILLER7\\n\\n*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\\n\\n**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \\\"Killer7\\\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\\nwith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\\nways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\\n`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\\n\\n**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\\nfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\\nfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\\nthem \\\"guests\\\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\\non rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\\nThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\\nbefore they reach you.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. How to use this plan\\n\\n- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\\n  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\\n- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\\n  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\\n  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\\n  art, music, levels or look.\\n- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\\n  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\\n  piece of content in this plan.\\n- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\\n  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. At a glance\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\\n| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\\n| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\\n| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\\n| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\\n| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\\n| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \\\"Turn-Down Service\\\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \\\"Après\\\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\\n| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\\n| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\\n| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\\n| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\\n| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\\n\\n| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\\n|---|---|\\n| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\\n| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\\\"THAW.\\\") |\\n| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\\n| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\\n| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\\n| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\\n| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \\\"Gratuity is customary.\\\") |\\n| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\\n| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\\n| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\\n\\n**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\\nnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\\n\\n**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\\n- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\\n  (its `<title>` starts with \\\"Skiller7\\\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\\n  holds a port.\\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\\n  the dev folder.\\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\\n  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\\n\\n**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\\n- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\\n- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\\n\\n`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\\n\\n| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\\n| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\\n| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\\n| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\\n| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\\n| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\\n| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\\n| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\\n\\n**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\\n```json\\n{ \\\"imports\\\": {\\n  \\\"three\\\":        \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\\\",\\n  \\\"three/webgpu\\\": \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\\\",\\n  \\\"three/tsl\\\":    \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\\\",\\n  \\\"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\\\": \\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\\\" } }\\n```\\n`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\\nnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\\ndouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\\nand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\\n\\n**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\\nhandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\\nconcatenates (§5.8).\\n\\n**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\\nfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\\nwe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\\nlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\\nengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\\nsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\\n\\n---\\n\",\"numLines\":140,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":2112}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc\",\"uuid\":\"8b95ea43-5b3c-454a-ac84-94a78e111f83\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174801__xc8e150/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1791,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1791,"newLines":6,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"1e7c72b8-2c40-4128-9775-9ffb8f791765\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":100,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"d2c52ed3-d3d5-4185-9807-ccbd364b00ff\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":250,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"e4821533-337b-45a3-bd68-74bc699b3a85\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":400,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"14e89baf-f0c3-460b-a064-883c4d44025d\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":434,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":34,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"0b4962d2-55f4-40ef-b836-9619025dbc77\"}","+{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2bch9z8BxJAsXrfAno\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2686,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":480131,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":2686},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"a6a22e8e-f9ad-4c1e-b19f-6f268b0ad619\",\"uuid\":\"21e0599a-b29d-45c1-9b94-5861a94fe7f2\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:34:41.816Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2bcSXHWRk7wndW4rTu\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":4028,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174807__x5c5240/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":2033,"oldLines":3,"newStart":2033,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":10650,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"7ca905d7-0f29-4d56-842b-589b8b900817\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":10800,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"45fa4f6b-5695-427e-9df1-de493a63ec0c\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":10900,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"1773993b-cf56-412d-8b7d-d0e2660d66bd\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":11000,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"3c819b0f-1b18-471b-b765-9dcf56869a17\",\"uuid\":\"9e000498-7844-4247-9ff8-a6d8fa559704\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174810__xbd1c73/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1943,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1943,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3600,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"38f072dc-7956-408b-966b-35dcd979a179\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3700,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"3601f749-8b32-4f9e-ae6c-d80497178809\"}"," {\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3850,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":150,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"77266f89-c48d-4408-a21b-1144352cb0ed\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":3950,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":100,\"session_id\":\"1d5c8bd7-2207-47fe-9b8b-c2b43fe30800\",\"uuid\":\"c6671408-d042-4ad5-b753-070138854321\"}"]}]},{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174825__x17a627/agent.out","hunks":[{"oldStart":1674,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1674,"newLines":4,"lines":[" {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh2aWDSM2Pv2sXbWUemo\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01WJK9rK9FxP3KEUPezqygRY\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174825__x17a627/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":1229,\"limit\":110},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6064,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":507745,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":6064},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8\",\"uuid\":\"29b28d31-1485-42c2-b280-6e3bc70bcc09\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:34:37.938Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh2aVvqX4wLQrrBATc2s\",\"wire_tool_inputs\":{\"toolu_01WJK9rK9FxP3KEUPezqygRY\":{\"file_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174825__x17a627/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":1229,\"limit\":110}}}"," {\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01WJK9rK9FxP3KEUPezqygRY\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1229\\t\\n1230\\t### 8.8 Story: *The Grand Kerfuffle* (5 chapters)\\n1231\\tThe writing style is dry, affectionate and digressive, like the commentary box; short lines; no line over 2 × 42 characters; markup for comic timing (`{w=.5}`, `{shake}`).\\n1232\\t\\n1233\\t**Prologue** (cutscene on the village green at dawn, after New Game):\\n1234\\t- AUBREY (narrating, as *The Kerfuffle Almanack*): \\\"Kerfuffle-on-the-Wold. Population: two hundred and twelve, and a cow called Doris.\\\"\\n1235\\t- AUBREY: \\\"Nothing of note has happened here since the hailstorm of 1953,{w=.4} which was rained off.\\\"\\n1236\\t- *The Phantom crunches across the square. Tyre marks on the pitch. Mrs Crumb drops a scone.*\\n1237\\t- THRUMPTON: \\\"Fourth of June, 1975. First ball. Given out leg before by a vicar{w=.3} who was looking at a butterfly.\\\"\\n1238\\t- EDIE: \\\"That was my grandfather. He still talks about that butterfly.\\\"\\n1239\\t- THRUMPTON: \\\"I have purchased the deeds to this green. On Monday it becomes the Thrumpton Parking Plaza.{w=.3} Four hundred spaces.{w=.3} No cricket.\\\"\\n1240\\t- MRS CRUMB: \\\"Over my dead sponge.\\\"\\n1241\\t- THRUMPTON: \\\"Unless... you beat my teams in the Grand Kerfuffle. Five matches. My grounds. My rules.{w=.3} Your umpires. I'm not a monster.\\\"\\n1242\\t- EDIE: \\\"Done. We'll play you anywhere.{w=.3} Bring cake.\\\"\\n1243\\t- *Title card: THE GRAND KERFUFFLE.*\\n1244\\t\\n1245\\t| # | Chapter | Opponent · ground | Scenario | Teaches | Beats | Kerfuffle Goals (stars) |\\n1246\\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n1247\\t| 1 | **A Gentle Knock** | Lower Muddleford · Village Green | full match, 2 overs each, KER bats first, Village difficulty default | Nets lessons 1–4 and 6: aim, ground, loft, defend, run, bowl | pre: Gaz's challenge (\\\"by warm-up I mean a thrashing\\\"); Nets with Mrs Crumb (\\\"Watch the ball, dear, not the cake\\\"); after innings 1: Thrumpton watches from the car (\\\"With the heater on\\\"); win: \\\"Beginner's luck. Thrumpton Hall next. Dress code: impeccable.\\\" | win · smash the pub window · hit a six |\\n1248\\t| 2 | **Tea on the Lawn** | Gentlemen of Thrumpton · Thrumpton Hall (overcast) | \\\"Kerfuffle posted 24 after a top-order collapse, chiefly due to peacocks.\\\" The Gentlemen need 25 from 2 overs: **you bowl and field** | lessons 7–11: aftertouch, Hype specials, catch ring, throws, reviews (the KRS appears) | pre: Peregrine (\\\"Do mind the peacocks; they bite\\\"); Fortescue announces tea \\\"at the fall of the third wicket, or at four, whichever is more dignified\\\"; mid: the ball comes back sticky (\\\"Is that... marmalade?\\\"), a review tutorial; win: \\\"I shall have Fortescue polish your trophy. With marmalade.\\\" | win · take a PERFECT-ring catch · win a review |\\n1249\\t| 3 | **Sand in the Sandwiches** | Sunburnt Bay Larrikins · Sunburnt Bay | chase 36 from 2 overs vs pace: **you bat** | bouncers (duck, hook), hard running and the dive, the tide | pre: the minibus breaks down and the team pushes it (\\\"Statistically, sunscreen doesn't melt at—\\\" / \\\"Mate.\\\"); mid: the tide warning; win: Shazza warns \\\"He's been buying ringers. Weird ones. See-through ones.\\\" | win · hook a six · win with a dive making the crease |\\n1250\\t| 4 | **Now You See It** | Magic Circle XI · Dustbowl Downs | full match, 2 overs each; a rain delay in innings 2, the Kerfuffle Method | spin (lesson 5: charge), stumpings, the rain delay, tea treats | pre: Spinaldo (\\\"Pick a card. It will be the card of your downfall.\\\"), Basil (\\\"I bowled leg-spin to a magician once. He sawed my stumps in half.\\\"); mid: rain, covers, the tea room scene, Mrs Crumb's chalkboard (\\\"carry the scone...\\\"); win: Spinaldo reveals the ghosts and the midnight booking | win · stump a batter · hit the hot-air balloon |\\n1251\\t| 5 | **The Midnight Final** | Thrumpton's Thunderbolts · The Megadome (night, fog) | full match, 3 overs each; the final over of the KER chase is the Thrumptonator's boss over; a Super Over if tied | ON FIRE emphasis, Triple Threat, ghosts, fog, the Super Over | pre: the Megadome (\\\"No tea. No cake. No interval. Just ADVERTS.\\\"); the ghosts rise (\\\"We have waited one hundred and forty-four years for a match that wasn't rained off.\\\" / EDIE: \\\"Then let's give you one.\\\"); mid: fog rolls in; the Thrumptonator wheeled out (\\\"Steam-powered. Never misses. Never gives anyone out leg before on a butterfly.\\\"); **win ending:** Thrumpton demands one exhibition ball against Rev. Hollis, who gives him out LBW first ball, again. Thrumpton laughs for the first time in 51 years: \\\"Plumb. Absolutely plumb.\\\" He tears up the deeds, the ghosts depart in peace (\\\"Worth the wait.\\\"), and the Almanack closes: \\\"Lord Thrumpton-Vane now polishes the tea urn on Saturdays and is said to be adequate at fine leg.\\\" Then credits. | win · survive the Thrumptonator's over without losing a wicket · hit the jumbotron |\\n1252\\t\\n1253\\t**Losses** (every chapter): a short consolation, e.g. MRS CRUMB: \\\"Never mind, dear. Even the best of us get a golden duck. Have a scone and go again.\\\" Then Retry / Retry on Village / Map.\\n1254\\t\\n1255\\tThe beats run as `game.script` generators on the bus (for example, `yield E.until(() => match.innings === 1 && match.phase === 'between')`, then play the scene). Story scene data goes through `E.Story` nodes, with speakers mapped to portraits.\\n1256\\t\\n1257\\t### 8.9 Lines\\n1258\\t#### 8.9.1 Commentary starter set (write ≥ 3 per event per commentator in this voice; A = Aubrey, D = Dazza)\\n1259\\t- **match start:** A \\\"Good afternoon from {ground}, where the sun is out, the tea is stewed, and {team} are about to play cricket, after a fashion.\\\" D \\\"Should be a cracker. Or a shambles. Probably both.\\\"\\n1260\\t- **dot:** A \\\"Defended. A stroke of great dignity and no ambition whatsoever.\\\" D \\\"Nothing doing.\\\" A \\\"Left alone. The ball goes by like a gossip past a closed door.\\\"\\n1261\\t- **beaten:** A \\\"Beaten all ends up! He could not have missed that more if he'd brought a map.\\\" D \\\"Played and missed. Dreadful. Lovely ball, mind.\\\"\\n1262\\t- **single:** D \\\"Tip and run.\\\" A \\\"A quick single, taken with all the urgency of a vicar late for a fête.\\\"\\n1263\\t- **four:** A \\\"FOUR! Struck with the elegance of a swan, and the violence of an angry swan.\\\" D \\\"Too short, too wide, too bad. Four.\\\"\\n1264\\t- **six:** A \\\"That is MAXIMUM! Somebody alert the air-traffic people.\\\" D \\\"That's gone into the next postcode, mate.\\\"\\n1265\\t- **six into the pub:** A \\\"Through the window of the Leaning Wicket! Last orders, I fear, have been called by the ball.\\\"\\n1266\\t- **greenhouse:** A \\\"Into the vicarage greenhouse. There goes the marrow competition.\\\"\\n1267\\t- **bowled:** A \\\"Bowled him! The stumps are doing cartwheels, which is more than the batsman managed.\\\" D \\\"Timber!\\\"\\n1268\\t- **caught:** D \\\"Up, up... and taken. Safe as houses.\\\" A \\\"A catch of quiet magnificence. Someone get that fielder a scone.\\\"\\n1269\\t- **dropped:** A \\\"Dropped! Butter, I think, would have had a firmer grip.\\\" D \\\"Oh, he'll be buying the drinks tonight.\\\"\\n1270\\t- **LBW:** A \\\"A huge appeal! The Reverend raises... raises... yes, eventually, the finger.\\\" D \\\"Plumb. Plumber than a plumber.\\\"\\n1271\\t- **run out:** A \\\"Yes! No! Wait! Sorry! The four stages of grief, conducted at a sprint.\\\" D \\\"Run out by a country mile.\\\"\\n1272\\t- **stumped:** A \\\"Down the pitch, through the gate, and the bails are off before you can say Kerfuffle.\\\"\\n1273\\t- **wide:** D \\\"Wide. Wide enough to land a plane.\\\"\\n1274\\t- **no-ball:** A \\\"No-ball! He's overstepped, which in this village is practically a felony. Free hit to come.\\\"\\n1275\\t- **ON FIRE:** D \\\"The bat is literally smoking. Someone fetch a bucket.\\\" A \\\"Willow ablaze! Health and Safety will want a word.\\\"\\n1276\\t- **special:** A \\\"Oh, he's going for something special. I can't look. I'm looking.\\\"\\n1277\\t- **review:** PRIYA \\\"Owl-Eye has it... pitching in line... hitting... and it's clipping the bails by a whisker of a whisker.\\\"\\n1278\\t- **rain:** A \\\"And down it comes. The covers are on, the umbrellas are up, and the crowd are discussing the weather, which is the only thing they love more than cricket.\\\"\\n1279\\t- **the cow:** A \\\"There's a cow on the outfield. A Friesian, I believe. She's at deep midwicket and, frankly, better positioned than their fine leg.\\\"\\n1280\\t- **idle filler:** A \\\"A Mrs Hepplewhite of Lower Muddleford has sent in a lemon drizzle. Mrs Hepplewhite, you are a saint.\\\" A \\\"There's a pigeon at short leg who has, I think, been there since Tuesday.\\\" D \\\"Lovely day for it.\\\" A \\\"Is it? I hadn't noticed. I've been looking at this cake.\\\"\\n1281\\t- **win:** A \\\"And that's the match! Victory for {winner}, for common sense, and for the scone.\\\" **loss:** D \\\"That's that, then. Shake hands, have a sandwich, try again.\\\"\\n1282\\t\\n1283\\t#### 8.9.2 Shouts (gibberish voice, captions in speech bubbles)\\n1284\\t\\\"YES!\\\", \\\"NO!\\\", \\\"WAIT!\\\", \\\"TWO!\\\", \\\"HOWZAT!\\\", \\\"OUT!\\\", \\\"CATCH IT!\\\", \\\"MINE!\\\", \\\"SORRY!\\\", and the crowd's \\\"KER-FUF-FLE!\\\".\\n1285\\t\\n1286\\t#### 8.9.3 Match report templates (results screen; fill from the ball records; pick by the shape of the result)\\n1287\\t- \\\"{winner}, set {target}, were indebted to {topBat} ({topRuns}{notOut}, {sixes} sixes, one of them through {targetHit}). Play was held up for {delayMins} minutes while {chaosThing} was persuaded off the square.\\\"\\n1288\\t- \\\"{loser} never recovered from {collapse}. {topBowl} took {wkts} for {runs} with a mixture of guile, swing and, in one instance, marmalade.\\\"\\n1289\\t- \\\"A tie, decided by a Super Over of almost unbearable tension, at the end of which {hero} was carried from the field on the shoulders of the scorer.\\\"\\n1290\\t\\n1291\\t### 8.10 Music\\n1292\\tSee §5.13.4. Sketch each song in the notation and audition it in the Nets (a dev-mode \\\"Jukebox\\\" row on the JUMP tab plays each song and sting).\\n1293\\t\\n1294\\t---\\n1295\\t\\n1296\\t## 9. Build order and milestones\\n1297\\tEach milestone ends with its tests green (`node test/run.mjs`), a bundle build, and a look at the screenshots it touched. Commit nothing outside `<dev>` except the final .html and the exit interview.\\n1298\\t\\n1299\\t| M | Name | Builds | Exit criteria |\\n1300\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n1301\\t| **M0** | Scaffold | the dev folder; the engine copied; vendor hub files; `tools/build.mjs` producing `dist/` and the single-file bundle with the client first; `test/run.mjs` running a first suite plus the bundle checks; `tools/serve.mjs`; a first title scene with the logo (finished in M4) | `node test/run.mjs` prints `N passed, 0 failed`; the bundle opens from file:// to the title; `engine/tools/check.mjs` passes on the bundle |\\n1302\\t| **M1** | **The Nets spike (feel first)** | the Village Green TileMap (basic floor texture), the broadcast and wide views; the batter rig with the **bat** (engine change 1) and pads; the bowling machine; the ball physics and predictor; the timing model and ground, loft and block shots for 6 resolver rows; the shadow, pitch marker and timing words; the cut to the wide cam; boundary detection (4 and 6); the TUNING registry and a minimal dev panel (sliders only) | unit-ball and unit-shots green; filmstrips of 3 shots and the bowling machine look right; a human can hit PERFECT drives reliably after 10 balls on County (play it) |\\n1303\\t| **M2** | Match loop | rules (§5.7), the delivery director, running (§5.4) with the AI partner, fielding (§5.5) for the CPU side, umpire decisions and signals (bowled, caught, LBW, run out, stumped), innings, target, result, toss, Super Over; the CPU batting and bowling AI; Quick Match 1P vs CPU with the human batting and the CPU bowling | unit-rules and unit-umpire green; sim scenarios for running, wickets and boundaries green; **bot-match** (CPU vs CPU, Village Green) completes with a result and no stuck balls |\\n1304\\t| **M3** | Human bowling and fielding, and the hub early | §5.2 in full (panel, marker, meter, aftertouch, over/round, field presets), catch ring, throws, keeper, bowler select, captain AI; **AI Hub parallel Super Over** (§5.17) with our card | sim scenarios for bowling, catching and throws green; **hub-mock** green; **browser-hub** passes with 1, 2 and 4 players (latency and loss); a full 1P Quick Match playable in both roles |\\n1305\\t| **M4** | Presentation | the camera director (all shots) and the close and review views; the recorder, replays, Owl-Eye, the third umpire and KRS; the HUD (§5.15.2); glyph prompts (§5.14.3, engine change 4); front-end menus, setup and pause items; settings; the 10-foot layout; the touch-only gate; commentary with captions; the gibberish voice synth (engine change 3); SFX, the crowd engine, music with layers | prompt and glyph tests green; HUD layout tests green on 4 sizes; browser smoke screenshots reviewed; commentary tests green |\\n1306\\t| **M5** | The arcade layer | Hype, ON FIRE, every special (§8.6), items, tea treats, stadium targets, chaos events, weather and time of day (fog, night lights), difficulty levels | sim scenarios for each special, item, target and chaos event green (forced via debug) |\\n1307\\t| **M6** | Content | all 5 grounds with their art (§6.3, §6.5), panoramas, props and palettes; the 6 teams and 66 players with rigs, portraits and voices; the story map, the 5 chapters, cutscenes, beats, goals and stars, the endings; the Nets lessons and Six Machine; Showdown Bat and Bowl with tables; Versus with 2–4 players; saves, unlocks; Wisden-style reports; all music | **bot-campaign** completes the story; bot-match on all grounds; local MP tests green; screenshots of every ground and kit reviewed |\\n1308\\t| **M7** | Attract, dev, about, polish | the attract showreel (10 segments) and its coverage; the full dev panel (4 tabs, every toggle and action); About/Credits pages; engine changes logged; balance passes with the dev sliders (aim: a County CPU chase of 30 off 2 overs succeeds about 50% vs a competent human); final bundle; exit-interview.json | **all suites green**, including attract-coverage, dev, bundle and the browser hub runs; no TODO or FIXME strings in `src/` (a test greps for them); the final reply written |\\n1309\\t\\n1310\\tWork inside each milestone in this order: simulation (with unit tests), then presentation, then scenario tests, then screenshots.\\n1311\\t\\n1312\\t---\\n1313\\t\\n1314\\t## 10. Risks and how to de-risk them\\n1315\\t\\n1316\\t| # | Risk | Why it is risky | De-risk now | Complete fallback if it fails |\\n1317\\t|---|---|---|---|---|\\n1318\\t| 1 | **Batting feel and readability** in an orthographic view | the depth and height of a fast ball are ambiguous without perspective; batting is the game | M1 first: the shadow disc, the pitch marker, the trail and length bands; the timing words; tune `camPitch`, `camScale`, `ballSpeed` and the windows with dev sliders; human playtest after M1 | raise the pitch marker to always-on with a closing timing ring (Statistical Certainty's visual) as the default \\\"Classic\\\" assist |\\n1319\\t| 2 | **Bat and two-handed shots on the rig** | the engine rig has swords, not bats; two-handed IK in the draw pose could fight the attack specs | engine change 1 in M1 plus a filmstrip of each shot family; put the left hand on the handle by IK *after* the attack pose; keep `hand: 'both'` specs | a one-handed bat hold (the left hand following loosely), which still reads as a bat at 40 px |\\n1320\\t| 3 | **Bowling action (windmill)** | there's no stock move for a vertical overarm arc | `solvePose` post-processing with an `E.ik3` circle over 0.18 s; filmstrip; compare with the `throw` move | the engine's `throw` move with a higher `z1` and a hop, which reads as overarm |\\n1321\\t| 4 | **Physics, predictor and AI coherence** | the AI, LBW, catches and Owl-Eye all depend on the predictor matching reality | one step function shared by the sim and the predictor; RNG cloning; unit test the match within 0.01 units | remove the seam noise from the predictor's input and add it as a separate post-bounce event the predictor also applies |\\n1322\\t| 5 | **Floor baking with big grounds and several views** | each view and scale bakes the floor; the cache clears past 6; zoom tweens re-bake | fixed views (no zoom tweens), raise the limit to 10 (engine change 6), warm the bakes in boot, chunked bakes; instrument `map.floors` | smaller grounds (rx 520) and only 3 views (close becomes broadcast with a tighter focus) |\\n1323\\t| 6 | **Stalled deliveries** (a fielder never reaches the ball, a ball never settles) | a stuck game is game-breaking | a timeout on every director state; the 15 s inPlay cap; the bot-match stuck check | already complete: the timeouts are the fallback |\\n1324\\t| 7 | **AI Hub conformance** | the client must come first; no blur pause; ready with no key; Enter on our card; time-up and end handling | M3 early; harness runs in the test command; `autoPause: false` in sessions; `card: true` handled with keys, pad and clicks | use the hub's own card (`card: false`), which removes the Enter check |\\n1325\\t| 8 | **Content volume** (5 grounds, 6 teams, story, lines) | it can crowd out polish | data registries plus the ground recipe builder; a squad generator; stars hand-made only; content in M6 after systems are final | fewer props per ground and shorter line pools (≥ 3 per event), never fewer grounds or chapters |\\n1326\\t| 9 | **Gibberish voice quality and clipping** | many simultaneous syllables could clip or sound harsh | a voice bus with a compressor (`DynamicsCompressorNode`); max 2 voices at once (queue); audition in the dev Jukebox | simpler square-blip voices with per-speaker pitch, still gibberish |\\n1327\\t| 10 | **The attract mode looping forever without errors** | long unattended runs find rare bugs | it uses the real machinery with cheats; the attract-coverage test runs 12 minutes; `game.errors` stays empty | fewer segments (keep ≥ 6, covering the coverage list) |\\n1328\\t| 11 | **Input conflicts** (J/K/L confirm and cancel, hub Create + Options, rebinding) | wrong menu behaviour annoys at once | a custom preset with confirm J and cancel L; engine change 5; tests for prompts and rebinding | — |\\n1329\\t| 12 | **Bundle size or an inline `</script>`** | a broken single file is a broken deliverable | the build escapes it, then a bundle test opens the bundle headless and in a browser | — |\\n1330\\t\\n1331\\t---\\n1332\\t\\n1333\\t## 11. Testing\\n1334\\t\\n1335\\t### 11.1 The one command\\n1336\\t`node test/run.mjs [suite ...]`, run from `<dev>`:\\n1337\\t- it runs every suite, or only those named;\\n1338\\t- it prints **only failures**, as `FAIL <suite> › <case>: <detail>`;\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8\",\"uuid\":\"9bebfe1b-e746-4bf1-8b50-150f7ff49bf3\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T01:34:37.943Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174825__x17a627/work/PLAN.md\",\"content\":\"\\n### 8.8 Story: *The Grand Kerfuffle* (5 chapters)\\nThe writing style is dry, affectionate and digressive, like the commentary box; short lines; no line over 2 × 42 characters; markup for comic timing (`{w=.5}`, `{shake}`).\\n\\n**Prologue** (cutscene on the village green at dawn, after New Game):\\n- AUBREY (narrating, as *The Kerfuffle Almanack*): \\\"Kerfuffle-on-the-Wold. Population: two hundred and twelve, and a cow called Doris.\\\"\\n- AUBREY: \\\"Nothing of note has happened here since the hailstorm of 1953,{w=.4} which was rained off.\\\"\\n- *The Phantom crunches across the square. Tyre marks on the pitch. Mrs Crumb drops a scone.*\\n- THRUMPTON: \\\"Fourth of June, 1975. First ball. Given out leg before by a vicar{w=.3} who was looking at a butterfly.\\\"\\n- EDIE: \\\"That was my grandfather. He still talks about that butterfly.\\\"\\n- THRUMPTON: \\\"I have purchased the deeds to this green. On Monday it becomes the Thrumpton Parking Plaza.{w=.3} Four hundred spaces.{w=.3} No cricket.\\\"\\n- MRS CRUMB: \\\"Over my dead sponge.\\\"\\n- THRUMPTON: \\\"Unless... you beat my teams in the Grand Kerfuffle. Five matches. My grounds. My rules.{w=.3} Your umpires. I'm not a monster.\\\"\\n- EDIE: \\\"Done. We'll play you anywhere.{w=.3} Bring cake.\\\"\\n- *Title card: THE GRAND KERFUFFLE.*\\n\\n| # | Chapter | Opponent · ground | Scenario | Teaches | Beats | Kerfuffle Goals (stars) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | **A Gentle Knock** | Lower Muddleford · Village Green | full match, 2 overs each, KER bats first, Village difficulty default | Nets lessons 1–4 and 6: aim, ground, loft, defend, run, bowl | pre: Gaz's challenge (\\\"by warm-up I mean a thrashing\\\"); Nets with Mrs Crumb (\\\"Watch the ball, dear, not the cake\\\"); after innings 1: Thrumpton watches from the car (\\\"With the heater on\\\"); win: \\\"Beginner's luck. Thrumpton Hall next. Dress code: impeccable.\\\" | win · smash the pub window · hit a six |\\n| 2 | **Tea on the Lawn** | Gentlemen of Thrumpton · Thrumpton Hall (overcast) | \\\"Kerfuffle posted 24 after a top-order collapse, chiefly due to peacocks.\\\" The Gentlemen need 25 from 2 overs: **you bowl and field** | lessons 7–11: aftertouch, Hype specials, catch ring, throws, reviews (the KRS appears) | pre: Peregrine (\\\"Do mind the peacocks; they bite\\\"); Fortescue announces tea \\\"at the fall of the third wicket, or at four, whichever is more dignified\\\"; mid: the ball comes back sticky (\\\"Is that... marmalade?\\\"), a review tutorial; win: \\\"I shall have Fortescue polish your trophy. With marmalade.\\\" | win · take a PERFECT-ring catch · win a review |\\n| 3 | **Sand in the Sandwiches** | Sunburnt Bay Larrikins · Sunburnt Bay | chase 36 from 2 overs vs pace: **you bat** | bouncers (duck, hook), hard running and the dive, the tide | pre: the minibus breaks down and the team pushes it (\\\"Statistically, sunscreen doesn't melt at—\\\" / \\\"Mate.\\\"); mid: the tide warning; win: Shazza warns \\\"He's been buying ringers. Weird ones. See-through ones.\\\" | win · hook a six · win with a dive making the crease |\\n| 4 | **Now You See It** | Magic Circle XI · Dustbowl Downs | full match, 2 overs each; a rain delay in innings 2, the Kerfuffle Method | spin (lesson 5: charge), stumpings, the rain delay, tea treats | pre: Spinaldo (\\\"Pick a card. It will be the card of your downfall.\\\"), Basil (\\\"I bowled leg-spin to a magician once. He sawed my stumps in half.\\\"); mid: rain, covers, the tea room scene, Mrs Crumb's chalkboard (\\\"carry the scone...\\\"); win: Spinaldo reveals the ghosts and the midnight booking | win · stump a batter · hit the hot-air balloon |\\n| 5 | **The Midnight Final** | Thrumpton's Thunderbolts · The Megadome (night, fog) | full match, 3 overs each; the final over of the KER chase is the Thrumptonator's boss over; a Super Over if tied | ON FIRE emphasis, Triple Threat, ghosts, fog, the Super Over | pre: the Megadome (\\\"No tea. No cake. No interval. Just ADVERTS.\\\"); the ghosts rise (\\\"We have waited one hundred and forty-four years for a match that wasn't rained off.\\\" / EDIE: \\\"Then let's give you one.\\\"); mid: fog rolls in; the Thrumptonator wheeled out (\\\"Steam-powered. Never misses. Never gives anyone out leg before on a butterfly.\\\"); **win ending:** Thrumpton demands one exhibition ball against Rev. Hollis, who gives him out LBW first ball, again. Thrumpton laughs for the first time in 51 years: \\\"Plumb. Absolutely plumb.\\\" He tears up the deeds, the ghosts depart in peace (\\\"Worth the wait.\\\"), and the Almanack closes: \\\"Lord Thrumpton-Vane now polishes the tea urn on Saturdays and is said to be adequate at fine leg.\\\" Then credits. | win · survive the Thrumptonator's over without losing a wicket · hit the jumbotron |\\n\\n**Losses** (every chapter): a short consolation, e.g. MRS CRUMB: \\\"Never mind, dear. Even the best of us get a golden duck. Have a scone and go again.\\\" Then Retry / Retry on Village / Map.\\n\\nThe beats run as `game.script` generators on the bus (for example, `yield E.until(() => match.innings === 1 && match.phase === 'between')`, then play the scene). Story scene data goes through `E.Story` nodes, with speakers mapped to portraits.\\n\\n### 8.9 Lines\\n#### 8.9.1 Commentary starter set (write ≥ 3 per event per commentator in this voice; A = Aubrey, D = Dazza)\\n- **match start:** A \\\"Good afternoon from {ground}, where the sun is out, the tea is stewed, and {team} are about to play cricket, after a fashion.\\\" D \\\"Should be a cracker. Or a shambles. Probably both.\\\"\\n- **dot:** A \\\"Defended. A stroke of great dignity and no ambition whatsoever.\\\" D \\\"Nothing doing.\\\" A \\\"Left alone. The ball goes by like a gossip past a closed door.\\\"\\n- **beaten:** A \\\"Beaten all ends up! He could not have missed that more if he'd brought a map.\\\" D \\\"Played and missed. Dreadful. Lovely ball, mind.\\\"\\n- **single:** D \\\"Tip and run.\\\" A \\\"A quick single, taken with all the urgency of a vicar late for a fête.\\\"\\n- **four:** A \\\"FOUR! Struck with the elegance of a swan, and the violence of an angry swan.\\\" D \\\"Too short, too wide, too bad. Four.\\\"\\n- **six:** A \\\"That is MAXIMUM! Somebody alert the air-traffic people.\\\" D \\\"That's gone into the next postcode, mate.\\\"\\n- **six into the pub:** A \\\"Through the window of the Leaning Wicket! Last orders, I fear, have been called by the ball.\\\"\\n- **greenhouse:** A \\\"Into the vicarage greenhouse. There goes the marrow competition.\\\"\\n- **bowled:** A \\\"Bowled him! The stumps are doing cartwheels, which is more than the batsman managed.\\\" D \\\"Timber!\\\"\\n- **caught:** D \\\"Up, up... and taken. Safe as houses.\\\" A \\\"A catch of quiet magnificence. Someone get that fielder a scone.\\\"\\n- **dropped:** A \\\"Dropped! Butter, I think, would have had a firmer grip.\\\" D \\\"Oh, he'll be buying the drinks tonight.\\\"\\n- **LBW:** A \\\"A huge appeal! The Reverend raises... raises... yes, eventually, the finger.\\\" D \\\"Plumb. Plumber than a plumber.\\\"\\n- **run out:** A \\\"Yes! No! Wait! Sorry! The four stages of grief, conducted at a sprint.\\\" D \\\"Run out by a country mile.\\\"\\n- **stumped:** A \\\"Down the pitch, through the gate, and the bails are off before you can say Kerfuffle.\\\"\\n- **wide:** D \\\"Wide. Wide enough to land a plane.\\\"\\n- **no-ball:** A \\\"No-ball! He's overstepped, which in this village is practically a felony. Free hit to come.\\\"\\n- **ON FIRE:** D \\\"The bat is literally smoking. Someone fetch a bucket.\\\" A \\\"Willow ablaze! Health and Safety will want a word.\\\"\\n- **special:** A \\\"Oh, he's going for something special. I can't look. I'm looking.\\\"\\n- **review:** PRIYA \\\"Owl-Eye has it... pitching in line... hitting... and it's clipping the bails by a whisker of a whisker.\\\"\\n- **rain:** A \\\"And down it comes. The covers are on, the umbrellas are up, and the crowd are discussing the weather, which is the only thing they love more than cricket.\\\"\\n- **the cow:** A \\\"There's a cow on the outfield. A Friesian, I believe. She's at deep midwicket and, frankly, better positioned than their fine leg.\\\"\\n- **idle filler:** A \\\"A Mrs Hepplewhite of Lower Muddleford has sent in a lemon drizzle. Mrs Hepplewhite, you are a saint.\\\" A \\\"There's a pigeon at short leg who has, I think, been there since Tuesday.\\\" D \\\"Lovely day for it.\\\" A \\\"Is it? I hadn't noticed. I've been looking at this cake.\\\"\\n- **win:** A \\\"And that's the match! Victory for {winner}, for common sense, and for the scone.\\\" **loss:** D \\\"That's that, then. Shake hands, have a sandwich, try again.\\\"\\n\\n#### 8.9.2 Shouts (gibberish voice, captions in speech bubbles)\\n\\\"YES!\\\", \\\"NO!\\\", \\\"WAIT!\\\", \\\"TWO!\\\", \\\"HOWZAT!\\\", \\\"OUT!\\\", \\\"CATCH IT!\\\", \\\"MINE!\\\", \\\"SORRY!\\\", and the crowd's \\\"KER-FUF-FLE!\\\".\\n\\n#### 8.9.3 Match report templates (results screen; fill from the ball records; pick by the shape of the result)\\n- \\\"{winner}, set {target}, were indebted to {topBat} ({topRuns}{notOut}, {sixes} sixes, one of them through {targetHit}). Play was held up for {delayMins} minutes while {chaosThing} was persuaded off the square.\\\"\\n- \\\"{loser} never recovered from {collapse}. {topBowl} took {wkts} for {runs} with a mixture of guile, swing and, in one instance, marmalade.\\\"\\n- \\\"A tie, decided by a Super Over of almost unbearable tension, at the end of which {hero} was carried from the field on the shoulders of the scorer.\\\"\\n\\n### 8.10 Music\\nSee §5.13.4. Sketch each song in the notation and audition it in the Nets (a dev-mode \\\"Jukebox\\\" row on the JUMP tab plays each song and sting).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Build order and milestones\\nEach milestone ends with its tests green (`node test/run.mjs`), a bundle build, and a look at the screenshots it touched. Commit nothing outside `<dev>` except the final .html and the exit interview.\\n\\n| M | Name | Builds | Exit criteria |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **M0** | Scaffold | the dev folder; the engine copied; vendor hub files; `tools/build.mjs` producing `dist/` and the single-file bundle with the client first; `test/run.mjs` running a first suite plus the bundle checks; `tools/serve.mjs`; a first title scene with the logo (finished in M4) | `node test/run.mjs` prints `N passed, 0 failed`; the bundle opens from file:// to the title; `engine/tools/check.mjs` passes on the bundle |\\n| **M1** | **The Nets spike (feel first)** | the Village Green TileMap (basic floor texture), the broadcast and wide views; the batter rig with the **bat** (engine change 1) and pads; the bowling machine; the ball physics and predictor; the timing model and ground, loft and block shots for 6 resolver rows; the shadow, pitch marker and timing words; the cut to the wide cam; boundary detection (4 and 6); the TUNING registry and a minimal dev panel (sliders only) | unit-ball and unit-shots green; filmstrips of 3 shots and the bowling machine look right; a human can hit PERFECT drives reliably after 10 balls on County (play it) |\\n| **M2** | Match loop | rules (§5.7), the delivery director, running (§5.4) with the AI partner, fielding (§5.5) for the CPU side, umpire decisions and signals (bowled, caught, LBW, run out, stumped), innings, target, result, toss, Super Over; the CPU batting and bowling AI; Quick Match 1P vs CPU with the human batting and the CPU bowling | unit-rules and unit-umpire green; sim scenarios for running, wickets and boundaries green; **bot-match** (CPU vs CPU, Village Green) completes with a result and no stuck balls |\\n| **M3** | Human bowling and fielding, and the hub early | §5.2 in full (panel, marker, meter, aftertouch, over/round, field presets), catch ring, throws, keeper, bowler select, captain AI; **AI Hub parallel Super Over** (§5.17) with our card | sim scenarios for bowling, catching and throws green; **hub-mock** green; **browser-hub** passes with 1, 2 and 4 players (latency and loss); a full 1P Quick Match playable in both roles |\\n| **M4** | Presentation | the camera director (all shots) and the close and review views; the recorder, replays, Owl-Eye, the third umpire and KRS; the HUD (§5.15.2); glyph prompts (§5.14.3, engine change 4); front-end menus, setup and pause items; settings; the 10-foot layout; the touch-only gate; commentary with captions; the gibberish voice synth (engine change 3); SFX, the crowd engine, music with layers | prompt and glyph tests green; HUD layout tests green on 4 sizes; browser smoke screenshots reviewed; commentary tests green |\\n| **M5** | The arcade layer | Hype, ON FIRE, every special (§8.6), items, tea treats, stadium targets, chaos events, weather and time of day (fog, night lights), difficulty levels | sim scenarios for each special, item, target and chaos event green (forced via debug) |\\n| **M6** | Content | all 5 grounds with their art (§6.3, §6.5), panoramas, props and palettes; the 6 teams and 66 players with rigs, portraits and voices; the story map, the 5 chapters, cutscenes, beats, goals and stars, the endings; the Nets lessons and Six Machine; Showdown Bat and Bowl with tables; Versus with 2–4 players; saves, unlocks; Wisden-style reports; all music | **bot-campaign** completes the story; bot-match on all grounds; local MP tests green; screenshots of every ground and kit reviewed |\\n| **M7** | Attract, dev, about, polish | the attract showreel (10 segments) and its coverage; the full dev panel (4 tabs, every toggle and action); About/Credits pages; engine changes logged; balance passes with the dev sliders (aim: a County CPU chase of 30 off 2 overs succeeds about 50% vs a competent human); final bundle; exit-interview.json | **all suites green**, including attract-coverage, dev, bundle and the browser hub runs; no TODO or FIXME strings in `src/` (a test greps for them); the final reply written |\\n\\nWork inside each milestone in this order: simulation (with unit tests), then presentation, then scenario tests, then screenshots.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Risks and how to de-risk them\\n\\n| # | Risk | Why it is risky | De-risk now | Complete fallback if it fails |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | **Batting feel and readability** in an orthographic view | the depth and height of a fast ball are ambiguous without perspective; batting is the game | M1 first: the shadow disc, the pitch marker, the trail and length bands; the timing words; tune `camPitch`, `camScale`, `ballSpeed` and the windows with dev sliders; human playtest after M1 | raise the pitch marker to always-on with a closing timing ring (Statistical Certainty's visual) as the default \\\"Classic\\\" assist |\\n| 2 | **Bat and two-handed shots on the rig** | the engine rig has swords, not bats; two-handed IK in the draw pose could fight the attack specs | engine change 1 in M1 plus a filmstrip of each shot family; put the left hand on the handle by IK *after* the attack pose; keep `hand: 'both'` specs | a one-handed bat hold (the left hand following loosely), which still reads as a bat at 40 px |\\n| 3 | **Bowling action (windmill)** | there's no stock move for a vertical overarm arc | `solvePose` post-processing with an `E.ik3` circle over 0.18 s; filmstrip; compare with the `throw` move | the engine's `throw` move with a higher `z1` and a hop, which reads as overarm |\\n| 4 | **Physics, predictor and AI coherence** | the AI, LBW, catches and Owl-Eye all depend on the predictor matching reality | one step function shared by the sim and the predictor; RNG cloning; unit test the match within 0.01 units | remove the seam noise from the predictor's input and add it as a separate post-bounce event the predictor also applies |\\n| 5 | **Floor baking with big grounds and several views** | each view and scale bakes the floor; the cache clears past 6; zoom tweens re-bake | fixed views (no zoom tweens), raise the limit to 10 (engine change 6), warm the bakes in boot, chunked bakes; instrument `map.floors` | smaller grounds (rx 520) and only 3 views (close becomes broadcast with a tighter focus) |\\n| 6 | **Stalled deliveries** (a fielder never reaches the ball, a ball never settles) | a stuck game is game-breaking | a timeout on every director state; the 15 s inPlay cap; the bot-match stuck check | already complete: the timeouts are the fallback |\\n| 7 | **AI Hub conformance** | the client must come first; no blur pause; ready with no key; Enter on our card; time-up and end handling | M3 early; harness runs in the test command; `autoPause: false` in sessions; `card: true` handled with keys, pad and clicks | use the hub's own card (`card: false`), which removes the Enter check |\\n| 8 | **Content volume** (5 grounds, 6 teams, story, lines) | it can crowd out polish | data registries plus the ground recipe builder; a squad generator; stars hand-made only; content in M6 after systems are final | fewer props per ground and shorter line pools (≥ 3 per event), never fewer grounds or chapters |\\n| 9 | **Gibberish voice quality and clipping** | many simultaneous syllables could clip or sound harsh | a voice bus with a compressor (`DynamicsCompressorNode`); max 2 voices at once (queue); audition in the dev Jukebox | simpler square-blip voices with per-speaker pitch, still gibberish |\\n| 10 | **The attract mode looping forever without errors** | long unattended runs find rare bugs | it uses the real machinery with cheats; the attract-coverage test runs 12 minutes; `game.errors` stays empty | fewer segments (keep ≥ 6, covering the coverage list) |\\n| 11 | **Input conflicts** (J/K/L confirm and cancel, hub Create + Options, rebinding) | wrong menu behaviour annoys at once | a custom preset with confirm J and cancel L; engine change 5; tests for prompts and rebinding | — |\\n| 12 | **Bundle size or an inline `</script>`** | a broken single file is a broken deliverable | the build escapes it, then a bundle test opens the bundle headless and in a browser | — |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Testing\\n\\n### 11.1 The one command\\n`node test/run.mjs [suite ...]`, run from `<dev>`:\\n- it runs every suite, or only those named;\\n- it prints **only failures**, as `FAIL <suite> › <case>: <detail>`;\",\"numLines\":110,\"startLine\":1229,\"totalLines\":1435}},\"tool_result_meta\":[{\"id\":\"toolu_01WJK9rK9FxP3KEUPezqygRY\",\"permission_decision\":{\"decision\":\"accept\",\"source\":\"config\",\"reason_type\":\"mode\"}}]}"," {\"type\":\"rate_limit_event\",\"rate_limit_info\":{\"status\":\"allowed\",\"resetsAt\":1793793600,\"rateLimitType\":\"seven_day\",\"overageStatus\":\"rejected\",\"overageDisabledReason\":\"org_level_disabled\",\"isUsingOverage\":false,\"unifiedWindows\":{\"seven_day\":{\"utilization\":0.41,\"resetsAt\":1793793600,\"status\":\"allowed\"}}},\"uuid\":\"540edb6c-86f7-4e7e-b7b4-58ed74e2916b\",\"session_id\":\"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8\"}","+{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"thinking_tokens\",\"estimated_tokens\":50,\"estimated_tokens_delta\":50,\"session_id\":\"10fd43c2-a819-4540-9aa0-cc285e3b03f8\",\"uuid\":\"c455f92b-f2ef-486b-a9